WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Hi, Jared.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.349
Thanks for coming, Jared.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.010
Did see you, or not see you

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.511
Jared, but glad to have you here.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.378
Stacy Novak says hello,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:21.860
Dr. Rosen.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.340
OK, it's--

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.379
Ah, hi Stacy, welcome.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.689
It's two o'clock, we'll
give it just another

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.250
minute. People are still coming in.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.140
OK, great.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.720
Up to twenty four people.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:03.830
That's pretty good.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.259
That's not bad.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.149
People tend to drop in and out as well with

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.150
these things.

00:01:15.623 --> 00:01:16.804
Give it just another
minute and get started.

00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:20.499
Yeah. Understandably.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.388
People are starting to, um,

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.309
experience soom extreme

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.410
Zoom fatigue, so

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.779
all these online events, so I wouldn't,

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:46.819
you know, you never know, I figure anybody,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.319
any more than 20 people is a good

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.050
turnout. Yeah.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.230
All right, we're almost at 30.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.780
OK, we'll go ahead and get started.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.540
Hello, everyone, thanks for coming.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.500
My name is Stephen Naron.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.079
I'm the director of the Fortunoff Video

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.795
Archive for Holocaust testimony and

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.464
testimonies. And it's
my pleasure to host this

00:02:08.519 --> 00:02:10.528
event marking the 10th anniversary of the

00:02:10.739 --> 00:02:13.049
publication of a wonderful book,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.304
The Wonder of Their
Voices, the 1946 Holocaust

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.691
Interviews of David Boder, published by

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.340
Oxford University Press
in December of 2010.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.859
Today, we're delighted to be joined by the

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.918
author, Alan Rosen, who will illuminate us

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.509
about the history and
ongoing significance of

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.519
David Boder and his interview project, as

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.739
well as representatives from the Illinois

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.139
Institute of Technology, the institution from

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.602
which Boder launched
his interview project in

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.720
1946, Adam Strohm and Mindy Pugh.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.150
Adam and Mindy help manage the Boder

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.515
collection and its online repository, "Voices

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.149
from the Holocaust." And they'll give us some

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.009
background about their effort to make the

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.620
Boder collection accessible and a current

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.169
joint project with the Fortunoff Video Archive

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.019
to make the two collections co-searchable.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.260
But before I hand things over to Alan,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.378
he asked me to say a few words about how I

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.579
view the relationship between the Fortunoff

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.540
and Boder collections, and how these two

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.929
very different pioneering projects, separated

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.645
by decades, share some common, some

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.820
commonalities.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.140
I'll begin with a reading of a memorandum

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.239
Boder wrote in May 1945.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.040
It was his preliminary plan for,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.429
for the effort to record DPs in Europe.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.918
Much of it, unsurprisingly, resonates with

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.939
the approach taken decades later by the

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.460
Fortunoff Archive's predecessor organization

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.979
called the Holocaust Survivors Film Project,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.280
or HSFP, which began in 1979.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.539
They're five bullet, six bullet points.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.030
And they go something like this:

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.529
"There needs to be the technical know how to

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:38.849
record human speech.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.039
The victims should be recorded in their own

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.929
language, in their own words, for

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.849
psychological and historical reasons.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.448
The victims' many exotic languages make it

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.439
impossible for journalists to do the job

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.440
competently.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.608
A psychologist must get the stories of all

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:53.890
the participants.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.429
The recordings can be used for documenting the

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.889
war and for educating Americans and Europeans.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.769
And six, time is of the essence since memory

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.239
and emotion fades.".

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.209
Immediately, it's hard to ignore more than a

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.340
few significant similarities between the HSFP

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:09.900
and Boder's memorandum.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.043
Firstly, the importance of the use of

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.919
technology to do this work.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.240
Rosen, in his book, writes, this clearly

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.239
applies to the Fortunoff Archive that, I

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.609
quote, "The history of Holocaust testimonies

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.521
has been bound up with technology, with

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.170
technological advance and obsolescence,

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.229
as a means to document or record testimony,"

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.230
end quote.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.189
Yiddishist Max Weinreich called Boder's idea

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.100
to record interview, "a happy idea."

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.719
This idea was followed decades later by a

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.480
group of survivors and allies in New Haven who

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.999
once again embraced new technological advances

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.129
to continue the work that had come before.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.600
It was another "happy idea" in

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.170
the service of documenting an unhappy subject.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.498
Survivors in their own words, this time on

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.454
video, recorded in the tenuous after

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:56.760
of the ongoing aftermath of the Holocaust.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.006
Some of the other bullet points in Boder's

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.660
memorandum also ring true.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.509
Another one is the desire to tell the story of

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.189
the Holocaust with the words of the survivors

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.248
themselves, to provide them a space to use

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.450
their authentic voice.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.069
There's also a recognition of the necessity of

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.981
expertise to do this type of work, both

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.285
linguistic expertise and a deep understanding

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.400
of the cultural context of the survivor,

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.739
something that both Boder and,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.649
as an European emigre, and the survivors

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.359
in the New Haven community could provide.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.320
They were themselves no strangers to the

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.840
European experience.

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.209
There's also a shared understanding of the

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.129
inadequacy of a strictly journalistic

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.237
approach, the importance of a psychological

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.359
framing and a recognition of the role of

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.789
trauma, an approach also taken by one

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:40.250
of the Fortunoff Archive's founders, Dori

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:41.519
Laub.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.289
Dori, himself the child survivor from

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.479
Czernowitz, did groundbreaking work in trauma

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.636
studies, had a psychological practice in New

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.409
Haven, and brought his own personal and

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.299
professional experiences to the project.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.059
The memorandum, Boder's memorandum, also

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.069
stresses the value of these materials for

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.650
historical research and other disciplines, not

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.329
exclusively historical research,

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.569
their potential documentation as well as

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:05.939
education. And finally, there is this

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.069
brutal recognition of the teeth of time,

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.199
even more acute for the pioneers of the

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.564
Fortunoff Video Archives, starting in 1979,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.550
after so many survivors had already died.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.937
Boder himself was a psychologist and, like

00:06:18.938 --> 00:06:21.299
Laub, saw himself as, I quote, "offering

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.029
a service that others behalf, on the,

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.502
working on behalf of the DPs could not

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.620
provide.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:28.949
After all, there were dozens of organizations

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.049
geared to alleviating the privations of the

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:33.629
present and assisting with rehabilitation.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:37.289
But Boder and Laub felt that psychological

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.389
tools and approaches needed to be the motor of

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:40.559
these, these efforts.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.545
Moreover, both Boder and Fortunoff's founders

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.199
felt that many previous attempts to document,

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.479
to represent the Holocaust were inadequate.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.569
In Boder's day, he was reacting in part

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.239
to the work of film crews, Soviet,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.240
British, American.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.409
The images that played in movie houses around

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.299
the world featured silent footage from the

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.260
camps. For instance, Majdanek, liberated

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:03.989
before the end of the war by the Soviets and

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.580
filmed by Roman Karmen and others.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.029
Boder saw this cinematic approach as

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.049
a failure

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.586
to document the European tragedy in its full

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:14.740
form.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.360
Alan quotes in his book, quotes a passage from

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.799
Jorge Semprun, his account, the

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.869
account of watching newsreels in '45.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.659
And he, Alan says that Semprun's words really

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:26.009
echo Boder's own notebooks.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.976
Semprun wrote, and I quote, "What was really

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.202
needed was commentary on the images to

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.580
decipher them, to situate them, not only

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.359
in historical context, but within the

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.889
continuity of emotions.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.379
And in order to remain as close as possible to

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.959
the actual experiences, this commentary

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.939
would have to be spoken by the survivors

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.135
themselves," end quote.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.679
Boder would seek to provide this commentary,

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.379
this soundtrack, using his wire recorder.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.499
And if Boder's work was in part counter

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.260
cinematic, so was the Fortunoff Video Archive.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:01.589
It too is established as a means to counter

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.599
prevailing representations of the Holocaust

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.600
and their failures.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.779
In particular around the time of the founding

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.103
of the HSFP, survivors cite

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.347
their distaste for the docudrama Holocaust,

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.129
released in 1978, as a catalyst

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.079
for this new video recording effort to set the

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.080
record straight.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:19.589
Their goal was to let survivors tell their

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.569
stories in their own words, like Boder.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.545
And I think Professor Hartman would agree

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.404
after all. He called video testimony

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.082
a new genre of documentation that was,

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.649
and I quote, "cinematic and counter cinematic

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:32.650
at the same time."

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.539
That quote's from Hartman's article

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.789
"The Cinema Animal" on Spielberg's Schindler's

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:40.102
List if you want to read more.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:43.379
One important difference between Fortunoff and

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.029
Boder was the scope of the interviews.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.669
Boder aimed to produce what he called topical

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:48.670
autobiographies.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.035
He was focused on a topical historical event,

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.280
the war and its impact.

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.580
His goal was not to record a complete life

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.305
story. Fortunoff, by contrast, felt

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.851
it was essential to begin with the survivor's

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.282
earliest memories, capturing firsthand

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.230
accounts of what came before the war.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.799
Fortunoff feels that one cannot grasp the

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.599
losses of the war without knowing what came

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.789
prior, as well as the events of the postwar

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:14.009
period and the reestablishment of lives in new

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:15.010
homelands.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.849
Nevertheless, Boder's use of the term

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.979
autobiography underscores an important kinship

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.959
with the survivor that is shared by Boder and

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:22.960
Fortunoff.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.829
There is an emphasis on agency in the term

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:28.110
autobiography. After all, Boder didn't say

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.330
he didn't call them topical biographies

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.696
drafted by a skilled, skilled biographer on,

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.000
on their behalf.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.179
Rather, the idea was to use technological

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:38.279
tools to provide the common man and woman

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.079
with a chance to record their own

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.329
autobiographical accounts, again in their own

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:43.330
words.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.959
In his notebooks, Boder wrote that when he met

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.209
with potential interviewees, they would often

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.415
seek to provide him with the most exceptional

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:51.990
and unusual stories.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.299
Boder would protest and tell them that he

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:56.554
wanted, and I quote, "wanted the rank and file

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.250
experience, not the usual story--not

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:01.370
the unusual story."

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.996
For a Boder, the goal was to produce a broad

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.789
record of dissimilar groups of individuals,

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:08.809
a wide ranging mosaic of stories from the

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.052
war, a goal the Fortunoff Archive also

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:12.680
shares.

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:15.509
Boder hoped the resulting interviews would

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.849
help explain to a naive American public

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.908
what the unnamed masses of DPs languishing

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:21.599
in Europe had experienced.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.789
In doing so, he had motives beyond

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.469
his own academic interest of studying trauma.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:28.349
There was hope that these stories could

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.389
convince the American public to open their

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:31.749
gates to further immigration.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.649
But beyond his political motivation, there was

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:37.259
also a keen sense of an ethnographic impetus

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.260
in Boders work.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.679
This is best demonstrated in Boders's many

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.780
recordings of songs sung by DPs.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.509
Capturing these songs, in Boder's opinion, was

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.579
the ideal proof of the value of the oral

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:48.580
recording method.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.449
Only audio or video in the case of Fortunoff

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.189
can capture songs adequately.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.709
Or, as one commenter on the Boder collection

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.039
said, it was a way "to

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.089
do justice to the oral dimension of the

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:01.315
victims experience." Once

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.819
again, this aspect of oral recording process,

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:06.659
the oral recording process is a common thread

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.277
between both the work of Boder and the

00:11:08.551 --> 00:11:09.551
Fortunoff Archive.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.868
In a way, they both mirror previous Jewish

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.187
ethnographic efforts, like those of Shimansky

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:16.529
and the interwar period, or the historical

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.530
commissions.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:21.569
And Boder's recordings

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.429
and Fortunoff testimonies therefore also share

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.769
a sort of longer legacy of a seemingly

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:27.289
endless effort by practitioners

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.027
of Jewish quote unquote, "salvage

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.020
ethnology." Serge

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:34.610
Kaczerginski, one of these salvage

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.140
ethnographers who recorded music among DPs

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.870
after the war, says it so quote,

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.514
"The songs the Jews from ghettos, death camps,

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.309
and partisans sang from their sad hearts will

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.109
be a great contribution to the history of

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.670
Jewish martyrdom and struggle.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:50.391
The songs present the daily Jewish life

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.900
in the ghettos with all its accompanying

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.139
phenomena like arrests, death,

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.839
Gestapo, Jewish power mongers, internal way

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:58.840
of life, etc.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.319
it's all reflected in precisely

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:03.203
in this bloody folklore."

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.231
I will note as an aside, that the video

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.309
archive continues to explore the elements

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.740
of poetry and song and its testimonies.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.989
And we have an ongoing project, in fact,

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:14.582
that's being led by our musician in residence,

00:12:14.583 --> 00:12:15.583
Zisl Slepovitch.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.406
And you can learn more about that project on

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:20.560
our website, songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:26.369
Finally, and this is likely the most obvious

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:27.779
connection between Boder in the Fortunoff

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.299
Archive is their position as postwar projects.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.909
They both share the rubric of belated

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:33.910
testimony.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:36.119
Fortunoff obviously more so than Boder.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.849
Belated testimony is often judged quite

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.889
differently in terms of value and accuracy

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:42.559
when compared to wartime testimony.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.713
But even Boder was aware of a lack of

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.309
immediacy in his recording project, despite

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.094
it already beginning in 1946.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.963
For Boder, in a way he had already started too

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.370
late. Months had passed since liberation

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.146
and this would indubitably alter the type of

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:58.949
story survivors would tell.

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:01.469
Still, Boder persisted in his effort as he saw

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.610
the work as essential.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.490
He recognized the lateness as a fact,

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.199
but he didn't feel that the work was

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.689
necessarily less important than that of other

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:12.059
groups like Oyneg Shabes, who documented

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.831
the Holocaust, slow burn and in media res

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:15.599
in ghettos and camps.

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:16.990
It was just different.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.500
The Fortunoff Archive shares this view,

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:22.161
despite its much more belated belatedness.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:25.492
And again later did not mean

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:27.809
worse. It just meant different.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:31.110
More importantly, in Boder's perspective,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.119
from Boder's perspective, the boundaries

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.429
between wartime and postwar, between before

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.390
and after, are less clear than one would

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:38.609
expect.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.099
And I believe we have Larry Langer on

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:42.670
here with us today.

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:45.569
I believe that Larry Langer and Robert Kraft,

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.759
two scholars who spent an extraordinary amount

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:50.069
of time listening to and studying video

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:51.830
testimony, would probably agree with him.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:54.479
They also found that the core of traumatic

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.636
memory persists and that there is often some

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.987
sort of bifurcation between deep memory and

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:01.919
common memory, the sense of the survivor

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.027
leading two lives, a normal life and a life

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.360
back there simultaneously.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.120
And as Rosen puts it eloquently in his book,

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:11.489
the boundary between the past and the present

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.829
is porous. And I quote, "Since

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.780
survivors in the aftermath of the war

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.013
have not yet subjectively experienced

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:20.351
a true after, hence testimony

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.571
after the war mirrored testimony during

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.579
it." These are just a few of

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:28.834
the most obvious commonalities and breaks that

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:30.689
I see between the Boder and Fortunoff

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.089
collections. They truly are like cousins,

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.639
divided by time and space, share

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.929
many goals and traits.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.419
This is why it's so edifying for us to be able

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.519
to provide a venue for revisiting Alan's work

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.480
today and to publicly discuss with Mindy

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.714
and Adam the ongoing joint effort to

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:48.149
bring Fortunoff, the Fortunoff Archive and the

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:49.920
Boder collections together.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.309
So with that, let's move on to our

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:54.400
main event.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:57.480
Our first speaker, Alan Rosen.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.141
Alan is an impressive scholar

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:01.740
with over a dozen books and innumerable,

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.469
innumerable other publications.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.209
For instance, Alan was the editor

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:08.819
of Literature of the Holocaust and

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.099
Elie Wiesel, Jewish, Literary and Moral

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:12.100
Perspectives.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:14.772
His latest book is The Holocaust's

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:16.772
Jewish Calendars: Keeping Time Sacred,

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.550
Making Time Holy, which came out in 2019.

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.739
That book was awarded the Yad Vashem

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.110
International Book Prize.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.710
He has taught at universities, colleges,

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.799
yeshivas, and seminaries in Israel and the

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.564
United States and lectures regularly

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:33.102
on literature and testimony at Yad Vashem's

00:15:33.156 --> 00:15:35.429
International School for Holocaust Studies.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.599
He's a native of Los Angeles.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:40.649
He studied under Elie Wiesel in Boston

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:42.299
and now lives in Jerusalem.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:44.490
We're delighted to have him here.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.040
Alan, you have the floor.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:54.209
But you'll need to unmute yourself.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:03.940
Some fifty

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:09.780
years ago,

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:14.563
in a different interview project,

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:17.706
a Mr. Slutsky described

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:22.910
how as a boy, a

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:25.969
teenage boy, he came back

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:27.849
from the ghetto one day,

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:30.209
found that his sister

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.969
had been taken in a round up.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:35.780
Mad with grief, he

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:39.280
ran and,uh,

00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:43.329
to the headquarters

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:44.330
of the enemy.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:48.529
And audaciously,

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.019
foolishly, dangerously

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:53.791
demanded that his sister be released.

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:55.599
They laughed at him.

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:58.409
And then the head of the

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:02.069
headquarters came

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:03.343
out with a,

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:09.100
with a mean

00:17:12.139 --> 00:17:14.299
approach to, to

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:16.908
Slutsky, said, I'll let

00:17:16.909 --> 00:17:19.279
her go if you

00:17:19.399 --> 00:17:21.530
grow hair

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:24.519
on your, on the palm of your hand.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:27.564
Mr. Slutsky then raised up

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:31.510
his palm, which was covered

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.110
with hair.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.490
The enemy

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:40.250
commander, furious,

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.519
grabbed the girl, threw her back

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:44.520
to the boy and shouted that

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:47.190
hey run out.

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:49.030
They were demons.

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:52.052
Mr. Slutsky, as he recounts this, says

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:55.149
that what had

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:56.830
caused the hair to grow

00:17:58.150 --> 00:18:00.639
was an accident that had taken

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:01.640
place.

00:18:03.370 --> 00:18:05.589
As a younger boy

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:08.680
and a skin graft

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:10.769
had come from such a hairy part

00:18:11.710 --> 00:18:12.710
of his body.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:17.559
The-- and then with

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.770
a, a

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.294
witty sense

00:18:23.410 --> 00:18:24.773
of fortune,

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:27.820
said that the doctors

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:31.320
told him that this was impossible

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:33.530
that a hairy part

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:36.780
of the body grafted onto the palm

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:38.997
should continue to grow

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:41.180
hair.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:43.960
But he says that

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:45.500
my hand never

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:48.609
went to medical school,

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.030
so it didn't know

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.940
that it was supposed to follow

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:56.720
those rules.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:01.099
In a similar vein, Boder came

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:03.740
early before

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:06.049
he had a chance to

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:08.545
have to weather what were seemingly

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:12.769
the claims that the

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.589
survivors spoke in

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:17.810
what is called, been called

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:20.610
an anti-redemptive way.

00:19:23.170 --> 00:19:25.720
So as I write at the end of my book,

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.119
which I'm beginning with here,

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:30.141
Boder believed in closure and

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:34.000
moreover a closure of a certain kind

00:19:34.570 --> 00:19:37.629
at the very end of his compilation

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:39.430
of 16 volumes,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.090
300-- 3,163 pages

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:47.280
entitled Topical Autobiographies.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:49.739
At the very bottom of the page

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:52.539
he had printed in Hebrew

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:54.836
script the word Hazak,

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:58.599
a flourish that was both surprisingly

00:19:58.600 --> 00:19:59.923
conventional and strikingly

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:02.840
innovative.

00:20:03.370 --> 00:20:05.740
Boder knew well

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:07.145
what he was doing.

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:10.899
The three cryptic letters at the

00:20:10.900 --> 00:20:14.019
end, he wrote to his friends, Frances

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.399
and Maggie Kaufman, in 1957,

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:21.049
allowing the German K

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:23.320
to creep into his otherwise admirable

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:26.629
English spelling, are the Hebrew

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:29.540
word Hazah, meaning be strong,

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.269
and they are imprinted at the

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.789
end of each book

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:36.409
of the Torah.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.889
And I then

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:42.799
perhaps can show here

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:44.976
at the end of this

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:49.441
humesh, where I'm pointing my

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:53.000
finger, doesn't have to be seen

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:55.374
in a very clear

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.649
way, but clear enough

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:02.584
to be able to know that at

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.319
the end of every sacred Jewish

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.440
book that hazak

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:10.500
is written.

00:21:11.060 --> 00:21:13.729
Just as it is at the end when we finish

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.819
one of the five books of Moses,

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:19.000
the, in the synagogue we call out, "Hazak,

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:21.060
hazak

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.539
v'nishazeik." "Be strong.

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:29.359
Be strong and be strengthened."

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.549
So that Boder

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:35.279
was here pointing to

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.799
the fact that he saw at the

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:38.800
end of his 10

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:42.149
years or so

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.480
of doing the interviews,

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:48.309
of processing

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.720
the interviews, bringing them out in a

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.369
book in 1949,

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:54.497
I Did Not Interview the

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:56.680
Dead.

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:58.830
And then spending

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:00.981
six years on his topical autobiography

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:05.460
that he saw this under the rubric

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:06.850
of Torah.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.599
And the closure that

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:13.149
he brought at the end was

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.519
to have it exist as

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.859
such, which did not in

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:20.170
any way mean that the

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:22.750
testimony that he

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.290
brought with the end was

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:29.050
anything less than devastating

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:32.480
to read and listen to.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:34.472
But at the same time that it

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.899
had a, for

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:40.579
him, sacred dimension.

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:42.849
The second comment

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:46.119
to introduce my remarks is

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:48.073
that I was approached a few days ago

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:50.884
by an author of children's

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:52.920
books, who would

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:56.579
like to render

00:22:57.150 --> 00:22:59.190
into a child's book

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.331
Boder's interview project.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.664
So he asked me, queried me,

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.860
well, what did I think would be the most

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:10.980
important aspect

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:12.728
to bring forth

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:15.028
in such a book?

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.790
And I responded, the fact

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:22.056
that someone,

00:23:23.430 --> 00:23:25.710
was not so young, fifty

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:28.139
nine years old, would travel

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:29.700
halfway around the world

00:23:30.540 --> 00:23:32.729
to listen to people

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:35.370
tell stories

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.920
of very difficult times,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.649
that act of listening,

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:43.799
which Boder did again and again

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:46.014
and again, not only in the interview

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:48.930
project itself in Europe,

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:52.499
but as he transcribed

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.839
the recordings over the next 10

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:55.840
years.

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:58.899
That he listened again

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.020
and again and again.

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:04.009
And that strikes me as

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:07.080
an avenue

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.440
to point children to,

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.589
who are perhaps

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:14.815
often taught about public

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:17.260
speaking.

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.389
But maybe not as much about

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:21.760
public listening.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:25.300
The third introductory comment

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:28.299
has to do with ten years,

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:31.149
and ten years since my book was published

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:32.882
and in the world in general,

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:35.737
ten years is seen, say, dedicated

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:38.349
to finding time,

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:41.799
we know that it carries perhaps

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:43.404
even deeper meaning within

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:47.960
Jewish tradition, the Ten Commandments,

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:50.923
in the Hasidic tradition

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:54.349
we talk about the ten powers

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:57.170
of the soul that emerge.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.389
And we've heard even in the little bit that

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:02.909
I've said that ten years were

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:05.549
important intervals for David

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:07.649
Boder in his project.

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:09.980
When once he,

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:13.460
once he was able to

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:18.580
go to Europe and

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:20.470
undertake to interview

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:24.763
from that point forward, he spent

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:27.220
virtually the next ten years

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:30.099
nonstop in trying

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.100
to find every avenue

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.079
to bring forth these

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:36.591
interviews to a public who he

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:39.490
felt were deserving

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:41.559
of not only

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:46.499
the superficial encounters

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.559
with the, those

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:52.260
who were murdered or those who had

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:55.559
survived that were in

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:56.834
the motion picture cinemas

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:00.930
at that time, but rather to hear

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.480
the stories of each and,

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:05.174
each individual,

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:08.550
different as they were Jew

00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:11.549
and non-Jew, child

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.490
and elderly, and everyone

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.829
in between interviewing

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.190
in some nine languages.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:24.839
So those that ten year period,

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:26.699
he also had

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:29.940
migrated to the United States in

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:32.700
1926, so a twenty

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:34.799
year period that passed

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:38.190
before he went on his

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:41.519
expedition, as he called it, to Europe.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:44.579
And so in thinking

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:46.589
about ten years, there are many different

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:49.049
meanings that coincide.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:50.050
And it felt me

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:53.681
that it was worthwhile to take stock of

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:56.149
what had taken place during that

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:58.800
those, those years since the publication

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:01.369
of my book to see

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:04.039
in what way it had

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:06.289
perhaps had some effect.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.319
And that's, I'll comment on

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:14.019
a, on those aspects here

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:17.319
today, tonight

00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:19.690
in Jerusalem, it's pushing

00:27:19.750 --> 00:27:22.750
9:30 at night in Jerusalem.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:24.957
So we're, we're sharing

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:27.940
many time zones here.

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:29.849
Thank you all for joining.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:32.060
And thanks especially to Stephen Naron,

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:35.740
who has convened us

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:36.980
here and done so

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:41.470
much to provide wonderful

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:44.739
programming at the Fortunoff

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.540
Archive, succeeding

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:49.145
Joanne Rudof, who herself

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:53.290
had done extraordinary

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:55.349
programming and accomplishments

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:57.129
there.

00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:58.130
And we're fortunate

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:00.680
to have them.

00:28:00.790 --> 00:28:03.089
And also, I

00:28:03.160 --> 00:28:05.499
had suggested that we might bring on board

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:08.859
the representatives of the Illinois Institute

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:10.183
of Technology, whose Voices

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:12.489
of the Holocaust

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:16.630
website, which is currently being

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:18.458
built, we look forward

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:22.119
to its launch, but in the time,

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:24.190
in the approximately

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:26.109
fifteen years

00:28:27.010 --> 00:28:29.349
or so of its been

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:32.020
up and running, has

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:35.560
been a, really set the standard

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:38.560
in my mind for all the

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:40.801
interview projects and providing access

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:43.690
direction in depth

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:47.059
that allows someone to work with

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:51.829
listening to the interviews

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:54.615
in both regional language

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:57.018
and in translation, so

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.190
we look forward to Adam

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:03.337
and, Adam Strohm's and Mindy Pugh's remarks

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:05.012
that will follow mine.

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:09.120
Thanks again.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.289
First, then

00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:16.643
to mention that since the publication

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:18.959
of the book, one of the aspects

00:29:20.170 --> 00:29:21.983
that has been surprisingly emphasized

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:24.583
to me has been Boder's work

00:29:25.569 --> 00:29:28.360
as an archive unto itself.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:30.050
And I don't think I

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:33.759
thought of it that way at the

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:36.249
time. But at least

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:38.649
two major conferences, one of

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:40.720
which I gave the keynote address,

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:43.210
Yad Vashem have

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:47.619
invited me to speak about Boder's work

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:48.620
in that way.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:52.570
And I think what it does

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:55.389
is to provide us with

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:57.879
a sense of the enormous

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.490
depth and depth and scope

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:02.134
of Boder's

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:04.796
interviewing that would have it be

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:07.930
its own independent

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:08.960
archive.

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:11.409
And I'll say more about

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:13.125
that at the end when I give a short

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:15.219
list of those

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:18.045
projects that I think could be done

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:21.940
to take Boder's work to

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:24.720
even a further stage and hope that

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.409
that will be taken up and

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:28.960
happen in the near future.

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:34.300
Second has been the technological

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:35.950
aspect.

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:39.910
Boder's keen sense

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:42.430
of bringing a wire recorder

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:43.790
to interview, which

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:48.760
was, is so astonishingly

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:52.780
seemingly unduplicated, had

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:55.989
Max Weinreich, as Stephen

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:58.330
Naron had mentioned, speak about his

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:00.327
happy idea and then say

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.382
it was, the only thing was that it was

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:05.579
surprising that no one else had done it,

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:08.649
and especially striking since

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:11.849
there was the use of recording

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.099
in Europe at the same time

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:16.770
in the DP centers,

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:21.470
but to record exclusively songs

00:31:21.710 --> 00:31:24.019
and not interviews.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:26.869
So that that technological side,

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:29.779
it's been a keen interest,

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:32.389
the University of Luxembourg

00:31:33.380 --> 00:31:35.046
and a team of researchers who have

00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:38.450
made a model

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:40.660
digital, a lesson of

00:31:41.180 --> 00:31:43.550
studying the Holocaust and also studying

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:44.640
the various stages

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:48.730
through Boder's, which Boder's work

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:51.109
went through, in,

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:54.030
in,

00:31:56.270 --> 00:31:59.119
in looking at the significance

00:31:59.360 --> 00:32:01.520
of what they call source digital

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:02.850
criticism.

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:05.779
I think for all that, that

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:07.260
Boder's

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:10.820
efforts ended up being most

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:14.389
strongly dedicated to print,

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:18.680
which is an irony, of course, because

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:21.350
we celebrate his,

00:32:22.490 --> 00:32:24.079
his making the recordings.

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:26.930
But he ended up

00:32:27.290 --> 00:32:29.395
to see that the circulating

00:32:29.990 --> 00:32:32.479
the print, in both his book

00:32:33.650 --> 00:32:35.560
and in the sixteen

00:32:35.960 --> 00:32:38.360
volumes of topical autobiographies

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:39.806
which he himself paid to

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.489
send to libraries throughout the world,

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:46.519
that that was a way to be able

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:48.380
to bring the

00:32:50.060 --> 00:32:52.699
interviews into

00:32:54.260 --> 00:32:56.839
circulation and to the notice of a

00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:59.539
range of different academics

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:01.570
and nonacademic alike.

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:05.869
With that aspect of print

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:07.879
has been the

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:11.059
publication of Boder's

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:15.319
book in a German translation,

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:17.859
which I was one of the editors with,

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:20.143
of, along with Julia Faisst

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:22.691
and, and Werner Sollors,

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:25.829
which came out, I think around

00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:28.020
2013.

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:31.470
And

00:33:33.590 --> 00:33:35.789
it's, it

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.570
was, its

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:41.149
success, limited though it

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:43.760
was, was very striking.

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:46.339
A review was written in the major

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:49.112
German paper, which caused it, at least

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:51.543
for a short time to be something of a

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:52.980
bestseller.

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:56.270
And more than that was

00:33:57.050 --> 00:33:59.198
Boder's, on Boder's

00:33:59.540 --> 00:34:00.667
wish list was they have

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:03.258
the book be translated

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:05.850
into German.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:07.650
So we by doing that fulfill

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:11.286
what was one of Boder's goals.

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:13.367
And he felt that Germany, because

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:17.479
they had been the perpetrators,

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:20.210
were in need above,

00:34:20.600 --> 00:34:22.909
perhaps more than anyone, of

00:34:22.910 --> 00:34:25.130
reading what these interviewees

00:34:25.460 --> 00:34:27.440
had said regarding

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:29.029
their experience.

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:31.460
And so that

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:34.159
that has been a

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:35.820
very,

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:40.660
a very rich dimension.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:46.079
One of the goals, I think,

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:48.439
would be to bring Boder's

00:34:49.020 --> 00:34:50.299
original English edition

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:52.439
into

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:55.292
greater circulation,

00:34:56.920 --> 00:34:59.259
whether it's a book to

00:34:59.260 --> 00:35:00.639
be published, which

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:02.858
I had already tried on

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:06.910
one level to do, and we

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:10.539
decided not to go ahead with that project

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:13.059
for various good reasons, but

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:16.119
another way would be to put

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:18.009
the book on a website,

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:21.309
and that would allow a far

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:22.310
greater readership

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.529
and also availability

00:35:26.530 --> 00:35:29.050
to researchers than the limited

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:30.632
availability of the book now

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:34.090
allows. And it's not just

00:35:34.390 --> 00:35:35.390
having the

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:39.739
eight interviews, four men,

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:42.409
four women, perfectly balanced

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:44.959
in that respect, as well as many others

00:35:45.380 --> 00:35:47.439
to be known in a greater way,

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:49.569
but it is also Boder's

00:35:50.810 --> 00:35:53.989
striking, trenchant introduction,

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.869
his commentary, short but

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:00.590
very revealing on each interview,

00:36:01.430 --> 00:36:03.050
to be able to be

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:06.650
that much better known.

00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:10.130
It could be done

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:12.409
with taking the

00:36:13.880 --> 00:36:15.889
fine commentary that went into the French

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:19.009
edition that I had the privilege of doing

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.262
with my colleague Florent Brayard

00:36:23.210 --> 00:36:24.337
and with his historical

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:27.320
commentary then being

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:31.459
translated into English or other languages,

00:36:31.820 --> 00:36:32.996
that would help to bring

00:36:34.610 --> 00:36:36.919
the historical aspect of Boder's work

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:38.090
to another level.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:42.750
Boder was not a historian,

00:36:43.890 --> 00:36:46.289
but was rather a

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:49.019
psychologist who, as Stephen

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:52.380
had mentioned, was very

00:36:52.650 --> 00:36:55.739
dedicated also to ethnography.

00:36:57.260 --> 00:36:59.619
So one of the most

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:01.730
central ethnographic

00:37:02.150 --> 00:37:04.730
dimensions of his interview work

00:37:05.510 --> 00:37:07.820
was to collect songs

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:09.320
and the place of song.

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:11.100
He began

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:15.259
to, at the DP centers,

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:16.699
he tells us

00:37:18.020 --> 00:37:19.294
with songs by asking those

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:22.070
who he met with

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:24.582
initially to sing songs that

00:37:25.610 --> 00:37:27.325
they knew from the war, from before

00:37:28.130 --> 00:37:30.529
the war, and that that would

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:31.679
loosen them up.

00:37:32.390 --> 00:37:34.760
And then during the recording sessions,

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:36.829
he was

00:37:37.550 --> 00:37:39.979
always attentive to

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:41.779
asking about the songs

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:44.119
and having them sung.

00:37:45.710 --> 00:37:46.710
And about midway

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:50.840
through, was so taken

00:37:51.170 --> 00:37:53.749
by what he had heard of these songs

00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:55.159
that he then

00:37:57.230 --> 00:38:00.140
orchestrated separate song

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:01.380
sessions.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.479
It seems that if we're to really

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:09.599
capture the ethnographic

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:12.140
genius of Boder's work,

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:16.050
we need to have those songs

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:19.259
not only available

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:20.850
if they are

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:24.539
in part on a website

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:26.939
for ORT and in a smaller

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:29.286
way on the Holocaust Museum

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:30.762
in Washington's website, but

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:33.319
to bring them together with the

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:35.290
interviews.

00:38:36.330 --> 00:38:38.610
Because if they exist

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:41.399
in separate realms, then

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:43.679
we've lost Boder as

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:46.139
the ethnographer and

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:48.449
we've lost the sense that the

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:51.119
testimony that he

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:53.900
sought, listened to,

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:57.029
solicited, also came

00:38:57.030 --> 00:38:58.696
in the form of the song, that that

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:00.579
was testimony no less

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:02.920
than the narrative

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:05.820
testimony that he heard.

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:09.289
So that would be a

00:39:09.290 --> 00:39:11.054
goal that has not yet been realized,

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:13.880
but we hope that both

00:39:14.210 --> 00:39:16.219
IIT and Yale will

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:19.159
strive to do that and hope to confer

00:39:19.190 --> 00:39:21.920
further on that side

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:24.289
of Boder's accomplishment.

00:39:26.389 --> 00:39:28.489
Boder was through and through

00:39:28.970 --> 00:39:30.169
a psychologist.

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:34.509
He went to undertake

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:36.176
these interviews as a professional

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:37.839
psychologist.

00:39:38.870 --> 00:39:40.438
In the first fifteen interviews,

00:39:41.210 --> 00:39:43.579
he used in the majority

00:39:43.580 --> 00:39:46.519
of them, the thematic apperception

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:47.850
test.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.840
And in that way, presented

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:53.989
his credentials as a psychologist.

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:56.580
He eventually

00:39:57.980 --> 00:39:59.499
jettisoned that test because it

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:02.989
took up too much time, he had

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:05.270
to hear other

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:07.699
kinds of recounting,

00:40:08.840 --> 00:40:10.442
but it revealed him

00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:14.309
as the psychologist who is

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:16.880
interested as he formulated

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:20.759
on the impact of catastrophe

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:22.358
on personality

00:40:24.110 --> 00:40:26.110
and devising

00:40:27.200 --> 00:40:29.480
as a result of that, what he called

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:32.389
the traumatic inventory.

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:36.329
There has been some follow up

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:39.449
to Boder's psychological work,

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:41.906
myself and my colleague, who I think is here

00:40:42.060 --> 00:40:43.285
today, Neil Lipsitz, have

00:40:44.490 --> 00:40:46.649
recently authored an

00:40:47.670 --> 00:40:50.129
article to that effect and I've

00:40:50.130 --> 00:40:52.230
written some others, and there,

00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:53.854
there have been, there has

00:40:55.020 --> 00:40:56.400
been attention paid.

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:59.480
But Boder's, his

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:03.889
actual project seen through

00:41:04.220 --> 00:41:06.320
a psychological lens,

00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:09.199
whole cloth, I don't think

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:10.983
has been pursued.

00:41:11.390 --> 00:41:14.090
Part of that is, of course, because

00:41:14.330 --> 00:41:16.639
the archives that would allow that

00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:18.920
to be seen in context

00:41:19.220 --> 00:41:21.790
are not altogether.

00:41:21.800 --> 00:41:24.770
There's one archive in Akron, Ohio,

00:41:25.070 --> 00:41:27.499
the American Association

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:30.860
of the History of Psychology, another at UCLA,

00:41:31.010 --> 00:41:33.349
some papers in the

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:35.749
horror-- the Holocaust Museum

00:41:35.750 --> 00:41:37.820
in Washington, others

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:40.489
in Yad Vashem.

00:41:41.770 --> 00:41:43.530
So that Boder's

00:41:44.980 --> 00:41:47.440
decision to go

00:41:47.470 --> 00:41:48.470
on this

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:52.450
expedition, motored

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:53.930
by his concerns

00:41:55.960 --> 00:41:58.509
and psychologist, will

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:00.749
need to be taken even a further

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:04.599
step than the chapter I

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:06.840
gave it in my book.

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:09.770
And I think what it

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:12.248
would bring is a sense

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:15.550
of being able to further

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:17.430
clarify why Boder

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:20.620
saw language in the

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:23.050
evidence of trauma that was

00:42:23.170 --> 00:42:26.019
in language, is such a central

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:27.020
concern, so that he

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:29.840
would, he

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:33.429
would only publish

00:42:33.700 --> 00:42:36.339
the printed versions

00:42:36.490 --> 00:42:37.764
of the interviews with the

00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:41.499
broken language, even

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:42.500
in translation, that

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:46.239
he felt had been

00:42:46.240 --> 00:42:48.880
the result of the experience

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:50.210
during the war.

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:52.405
That that dimension still

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:56.009
needs to be brought out in

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:57.840
a way, I think, too,

00:42:58.740 --> 00:42:59.916
for us to appreciate the

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.849
depth of the

00:43:03.630 --> 00:43:06.570
scars that were left

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:09.509
during the war and continued on

00:43:09.960 --> 00:43:10.960
after.

00:43:13.440 --> 00:43:16.229
What has also come is

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:19.559
the popularization

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:22.500
of Boder's work, not only the

00:43:22.890 --> 00:43:25.469
project of a child's book,

00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:26.886
which is hopefully going

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:29.050
to come, but also in

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:33.599
several radio programs that BBC

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:35.939
and NPR also in a

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:38.040
novel by Elliott Perlman,

00:43:38.370 --> 00:43:40.409
published in 2011

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.820
called The Street Sweeper,

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:47.699
where as novelists will do,

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:49.500
he took liberties.

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:51.900
And to

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:55.710
make the story a compelling one,

00:43:56.270 --> 00:43:58.299
as if it seems to me Boder's

00:43:58.980 --> 00:44:00.950
story wasn't compelling

00:44:01.320 --> 00:44:03.150
enough in its

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:04.840
facts.

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:07.679
But he had a good deal

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:08.680
of success.

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:11.396
But in a way left a,

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:12.759
a larger swath of the

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:16.070
public with a misrepresentation

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:20.159
of Boder as a loner

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:23.429
who had engineered this project

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:25.489
on his own with the,

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:29.009
with the uncaring

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:32.099
responses of those who would not

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:33.100
support him.

00:44:34.330 --> 00:44:35.533
And what I found,

00:44:36.640 --> 00:44:38.979
nothing could be further from the

00:44:38.980 --> 00:44:40.303
truth. Boder was constantly

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:44.050
supported by friends,

00:44:44.170 --> 00:44:47.319
family, colleagues, academics,

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:48.599
the world wide.

00:44:48.880 --> 00:44:50.399
He was funded for the ten years

00:44:51.370 --> 00:44:52.749
he worked on the project.

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:55.750
The National Institute of Mental Health,

00:44:56.530 --> 00:44:58.469
who saw his work, has been,

00:45:00.070 --> 00:45:01.810
has been certainly

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:05.079
worth supporting in every respect

00:45:05.530 --> 00:45:07.899
and it goes on from

00:45:07.900 --> 00:45:08.900
there.

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:11.879
Most recently, as a,

00:45:12.090 --> 00:45:14.969
as a very intriguing

00:45:15.480 --> 00:45:16.798
offshoot of the popularization,

00:45:18.030 --> 00:45:20.340
has been a film

00:45:20.580 --> 00:45:22.649
done on Boder's

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:25.154
daughter, Elena Boder,

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:28.679
who was a pediatrician

00:45:29.190 --> 00:45:32.039
who made striking breakthroughs

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:34.770
in diagnosing

00:45:34.890 --> 00:45:35.890
a very terrible

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:38.839
child's illness, but allowed it

00:45:39.630 --> 00:45:41.999
to be treated in a better way

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:44.340
and also in

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:47.850
a pathbreaker pioneering in

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:50.030
dealing with dyslexia.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:54.110
And this

00:45:55.880 --> 00:45:58.009
brings to the fore

00:45:58.190 --> 00:46:01.309
Boder's biography, but unfortunately,

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:04.130
the historical backdrop

00:46:04.610 --> 00:46:05.980
is misrepresented

00:46:08.060 --> 00:46:09.259
facts or wrong.

00:46:11.090 --> 00:46:12.090
And what we have

00:46:13.520 --> 00:46:16.310
of Boder's life is only

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:18.250
also backdrop.

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:21.367
And we're still waiting, I think,

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:24.639
for the, the kind of

00:46:24.640 --> 00:46:26.460
popularization, if it's to come,

00:46:27.370 --> 00:46:29.469
that will deal with Boder

00:46:29.890 --> 00:46:31.720
as a,

00:46:32.350 --> 00:46:35.199
as his life story,

00:46:35.200 --> 00:46:37.111
which is transparent virtually in every

00:46:37.630 --> 00:46:39.969
respect and extraordinary

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:41.930
in its epic dimensions that it

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:44.290
really deserves.

00:46:49.800 --> 00:46:50.800
An aspect

00:46:54.930 --> 00:46:57.270
of the project that has been dealt with,

00:46:58.110 --> 00:46:59.384
but hopefully will receive

00:47:00.750 --> 00:47:03.599
more is the reinterviewing

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:05.909
of Boder interviewees that

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:07.650
was done in the U.S.

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:10.409
at the United States

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:13.229
Holocaust Museum in Washington under

00:47:13.230 --> 00:47:15.435
the directorship of Joan Ringelheim, with the

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:17.083
help of Elizabeth Hedlund

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:21.139
from approximately 2002

00:47:21.220 --> 00:47:23.909
to mainly,

00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:25.282
I think around 2007, just as

00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:27.339
I was getting off the

00:47:28.650 --> 00:47:30.840
ground and working on the project.

00:47:30.870 --> 00:47:33.150
So it was a great help to me.

00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:35.730
And these interviews are

00:47:35.940 --> 00:47:37.116
wonderfully available on

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:40.889
the museum's

00:47:40.890 --> 00:47:41.890
website, 10 of

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.689
them, and along with the

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:46.949
transcriptions

00:47:48.250 --> 00:47:49.250
in either

00:47:50.820 --> 00:47:53.279
English as most of the original

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:54.652
interviewing was done or, in

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:56.864
the case of several Hebrew

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:00.959
interviews, with an English translation.

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:04.480
Just a, a,

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:08.679
an aspect show what is

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:09.807
possible that's brought

00:48:11.080 --> 00:48:13.120
to us by way of

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:15.742
Sharon, Sharon

00:48:15.910 --> 00:48:17.478
Kangisser Cohen's book Testimony

00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:20.987
And Time, which is a,

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:22.610
is a first

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:25.989
book really on the

00:48:26.380 --> 00:48:27.550
reinterviewing.

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:30.099
She brings the

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:33.460
interview that David Boder did

00:48:33.820 --> 00:48:35.001
with Gert

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:39.640
Silberbard, where

00:48:39.670 --> 00:48:41.590
he arrives at

00:48:42.430 --> 00:48:43.430
Birkenau.

00:48:43.630 --> 00:48:46.420
And Boder says "yes

00:48:46.660 --> 00:48:48.999
now, and tell me how, what happened in

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:51.789
the first two hours after the train

00:48:52.150 --> 00:48:53.890
had arrived in Auschwitz."

00:48:54.760 --> 00:48:56.622
So Gert Silberbard says, "we were all,

00:48:57.070 --> 00:48:59.440
we were all unloaded from the car.

00:48:59.740 --> 00:49:01.989
And immediately a loud voice called

00:49:02.200 --> 00:49:04.062
'women, men, women to the left, men to

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:06.999
the right.' I stood with the men in

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:08.519
the dark. I had, we had arrived

00:49:09.610 --> 00:49:11.949
late in the evening and

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:13.175
it was already very dark.

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:16.689
Beginning of March.

00:49:16.840 --> 00:49:18.604
It was dark. And I had lost track of

00:49:19.360 --> 00:49:21.124
father and mother and son, no member

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:24.670
of my family anymore." Boder:

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:26.813
"So you were saying that immediately,

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:28.970
immediately, you did not see your father

00:49:29.050 --> 00:49:31.350
anymore either." Gert

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:34.149
Silberbard: "No, I did not see any

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:36.220
of my family anymore."

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:39.272
That's from his interview of summer

00:49:40.810 --> 00:49:42.340
1946 in German.

00:49:42.820 --> 00:49:45.279
And interviewed in Australia in

00:49:46.060 --> 00:49:47.748
2006, the interviewer

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:51.670
says, "So you arrived in, in,

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:54.107
did you, did you, how did you know you were

00:49:54.340 --> 00:49:56.409
in Auschwitz?" Gert

00:49:56.770 --> 00:49:58.338
Silberbard, who then has changed

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:01.980
his name to Gerhard Silver: "I, I

00:50:01.990 --> 00:50:03.100
found out later.

00:50:03.370 --> 00:50:05.739
I didn't know where I was when we,

00:50:06.040 --> 00:50:08.529
when we disembarked, but I found

00:50:08.530 --> 00:50:11.169
out fairly soon afterwards where I was.

00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:13.750
When we arrived, we were asked to leave the

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:15.093
wagons. There was an SS man

00:50:16.090 --> 00:50:17.756
on the podium and they said 'women

00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:20.325
and children to the left and men to

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:23.289
the right.' And I instinctively

00:50:23.290 --> 00:50:25.989
held onto my mother and joined her

00:50:25.990 --> 00:50:28.660
and my sister, two women

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:31.029
to the left, where the women and the children

00:50:31.030 --> 00:50:32.030
were.

00:50:32.110 --> 00:50:34.119
And then, my mother

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:36.370
pushed me to the men.

00:50:37.670 --> 00:50:38.699
So I think she really

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:42.890
gave birth to me twice,

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:45.825
once in 1928

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:48.850
and another time in 1943.

00:50:51.340 --> 00:50:53.620
My sister and my mother

00:50:54.100 --> 00:50:56.234
went straight to the gas chambers."

00:50:57.700 --> 00:50:59.770
So this reinterviewing

00:51:00.460 --> 00:51:01.495
then allows

00:51:02.849 --> 00:51:03.927
Gert Silberbard to not

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:07.560
only render

00:51:07.950 --> 00:51:10.409
a chronicle of what happened on

00:51:10.410 --> 00:51:12.570
arrival, but to

00:51:13.350 --> 00:51:15.770
reinterpret for us his

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:17.909
own sentiments that have come

00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:21.840
after some

00:51:22.080 --> 00:51:24.000
60 years later

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:27.269
when he looks back

00:51:27.270 --> 00:51:28.270
on that,

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:33.949
that event and can see

00:51:34.310 --> 00:51:35.965
himself twice-born.

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:40.340
As I move to the conclusion,

00:51:42.530 --> 00:51:43.530
a

00:51:46.709 --> 00:51:48.718
translation, as we just heard there, from

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:50.893
German to English,

00:51:52.090 --> 00:51:54.880
the tension and translation

00:51:55.390 --> 00:51:57.699
that I looked at in the section of my

00:51:57.700 --> 00:51:59.320
book has been

00:52:00.160 --> 00:52:02.259
really secondary,

00:52:02.920 --> 00:52:05.020
even though I think Boder

00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:06.519
who was primary.

00:52:07.680 --> 00:52:09.960
Boder, as I comment,

00:52:10.440 --> 00:52:12.870
for his work in Los Angeles

00:52:13.320 --> 00:52:15.870
on the transcriptions,

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:17.482
felt that he himself was out

00:52:18.540 --> 00:52:20.670
of his league and

00:52:20.970 --> 00:52:23.009
hired Bernard Wolf,

00:52:23.490 --> 00:52:25.919
Polish Jewish survivor,

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:28.170
to do the translation

00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:31.590
from Yiddish, Polish,

00:52:31.890 --> 00:52:32.890
German to

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:37.469
I think it was some twenty seven

00:52:37.770 --> 00:52:38.900
interviews.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:42.779
So being able to see

00:52:43.200 --> 00:52:45.690
what was involved with that

00:52:46.350 --> 00:52:49.230
move to a translation

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:51.989
that had to be done by

00:52:52.080 --> 00:52:53.256
a survivor, seen it from

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:56.060
inside and Boder

00:52:57.330 --> 00:52:58.800
yielding the floor and our way

00:52:59.820 --> 00:53:01.489
of thinking of translation.

00:53:01.500 --> 00:53:04.229
There have been a number of books

00:53:04.230 --> 00:53:06.719
done on Holocaust

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:09.509
studies in translation, but I

00:53:09.510 --> 00:53:11.789
think this is moving

00:53:11.820 --> 00:53:14.039
to a dimension of Boder's work

00:53:15.160 --> 00:53:17.560
that has been largely

00:53:17.710 --> 00:53:20.079
overshadowed by other concerns.

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:25.489
The bringing together

00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:28.459
of the Boder archive into

00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:29.538
the Fortunoff, merging

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:34.300
them together, it's, I think, pathbreaking.

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:37.501
It realizes what I think Geoffrey Hartman

00:53:37.780 --> 00:53:40.079
and Dori Laub would have

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:41.230
celebrated.

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:44.009
Dori Laub, when he was made

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:46.534
aware of the Boder interviews,

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:50.739
was so

00:53:51.160 --> 00:53:53.889
overwhelmed by what he talked

00:53:53.890 --> 00:53:55.869
about as being the rawness,

00:53:56.350 --> 00:53:57.869
and our discussions were one of

00:53:58.690 --> 00:54:01.449
the most moving aspects

00:54:01.450 --> 00:54:03.165
of the work on the Boder interviews

00:54:03.850 --> 00:54:04.850
for me.

00:54:04.990 --> 00:54:07.520
So having the Boder recordings find

00:54:07.570 --> 00:54:09.250
a second home

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:12.210
in Yale is extraordinary.

00:54:16.170 --> 00:54:18.949
So finally, to

00:54:18.950 --> 00:54:21.379
the to-dos, besides

00:54:21.500 --> 00:54:23.659
those that I mentioned, would be,

00:54:24.080 --> 00:54:26.239
perhaps, to follow in

00:54:26.450 --> 00:54:28.459
Boder's footsteps, to take the expedition

00:54:29.210 --> 00:54:31.969
again and for some enterprising

00:54:33.170 --> 00:54:34.346
younger soul, or perhaps

00:54:35.510 --> 00:54:36.931
not so younger soul, to go to

00:54:38.150 --> 00:54:40.279
each of the centers in

00:54:41.180 --> 00:54:43.279
France, Italy, Switzerland,

00:54:43.530 --> 00:54:45.245
Germany, which of course, no longer

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:46.860
there.

00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:49.099
But the simple retracing

00:54:49.490 --> 00:54:52.699
may be able to unleash a sense of perspective

00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:54.127
of what Boder was doing

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:57.949
that we still haven't been able

00:54:58.160 --> 00:54:59.160
to see.

00:54:59.480 --> 00:55:00.558
Axel Doßmann, a German

00:55:02.150 --> 00:55:04.459
researcher with great dedication to

00:55:04.660 --> 00:55:06.949
Boder's work, has done some of that,

00:55:07.490 --> 00:55:10.240
but it still is there to continue.

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:14.929
In addition to bringing out

00:55:15.260 --> 00:55:16.429
in available,

00:55:18.080 --> 00:55:20.260
into an available venue, Boder's

00:55:20.660 --> 00:55:23.029
book, I Did Not Interview the Dead, I

00:55:23.030 --> 00:55:25.130
think we need to do that with his

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:27.139
two major articles.

00:55:27.380 --> 00:55:29.090
We need to do that with

00:55:30.230 --> 00:55:32.599
the, if possible, his

00:55:32.600 --> 00:55:33.920
16 volume

00:55:35.150 --> 00:55:37.279
topical autobiography.

00:55:38.450 --> 00:55:40.369
And in that way

00:55:40.880 --> 00:55:43.969
that the Boder library

00:55:43.970 --> 00:55:45.864
will be open, the Boder

00:55:46.430 --> 00:55:47.893
archive has since been done.

00:55:51.790 --> 00:55:53.842
To end, I'll end with

00:55:55.660 --> 00:55:58.179
what I've been ending most of my lectures

00:55:58.660 --> 00:55:59.660
with on Boder.

00:56:01.270 --> 00:56:03.429
Which is a story that tries to bring into

00:56:03.430 --> 00:56:05.105
perspective the

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:09.260
more,

00:56:11.410 --> 00:56:12.410
more overarching meaning.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:17.449
And I'll tell it in the shortest way possible.

00:56:19.170 --> 00:56:20.787
In about something over 200 years

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:24.029
ago, there was a rabbi,

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:26.348
Shmuel Solomon of Lyady, who was

00:56:27.090 --> 00:56:29.009
imprisoned in

00:56:29.670 --> 00:56:30.719
St. Petersburg.

00:56:32.150 --> 00:56:33.473
During the time, the warden

00:56:34.820 --> 00:56:36.979
found out that they had a scholar there

00:56:37.400 --> 00:56:39.019
and came to see the rabbi

00:56:39.770 --> 00:56:41.809
to ask if he could,

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:46.099
he could ask him a question

00:56:46.100 --> 00:56:47.100
about the Bible.

00:56:48.110 --> 00:56:50.329
Rabbi said, of course, so

00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:52.211
the warden asked, in the Book

00:56:53.300 --> 00:56:55.820
of Genesis, we find that after

00:56:57.020 --> 00:56:59.360
Adam and Eve ate from the

00:56:59.630 --> 00:57:01.247
forbidden fruit, they ran to hide

00:57:02.090 --> 00:57:03.949
behind a bush.

00:57:05.300 --> 00:57:07.670
And God, the

00:57:07.910 --> 00:57:10.670
Book of Genesis tells us, came

00:57:11.300 --> 00:57:13.999
into the garden and,

00:57:15.080 --> 00:57:17.326
and called out, Ayeka,

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:19.590
where are you?

00:57:20.090 --> 00:57:22.159
So the warden asked the rabbi

00:57:22.610 --> 00:57:25.159
what kind of, what kind

00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:26.660
of proposition is this?

00:57:26.930 --> 00:57:29.659
God who's all seen, who's omniscient

00:57:30.170 --> 00:57:32.570
can, is calling out, where

00:57:32.600 --> 00:57:35.219
are you? You can't see them behind

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:37.050
the bush that they're hiding.

00:57:37.370 --> 00:57:39.769
So the rabbi says to the warden, do you

00:57:39.770 --> 00:57:42.279
believe that the Bible is an eternal book?

00:57:42.530 --> 00:57:44.779
Yes, said the warden, was a religious

00:57:44.780 --> 00:57:47.329
Christian. Do you believe that

00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:49.699
then it pertains to each and every one of

00:57:50.090 --> 00:57:52.070
us? Yes, said the warden.

00:57:53.030 --> 00:57:55.579
So the rabbi says, I'll tell you what God

00:57:55.580 --> 00:57:57.910
was saying when he called out ayeka,

00:57:58.430 --> 00:57:59.430
where are you?

00:57:59.450 --> 00:58:01.759
He was saying that Adam and Eve, where

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:04.029
are you, not where are you hiding?

00:58:04.790 --> 00:58:06.259
But now that you've

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:09.679
enacted this deed, which

00:58:09.680 --> 00:58:12.250
has changed the face of all

00:58:12.260 --> 00:58:13.583
the world, where are you in

00:58:14.900 --> 00:58:16.539
your course of life?

00:58:18.810 --> 00:58:21.239
So as I end

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:22.710
with this story and lecture, I

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:26.099
say, this is

00:58:26.100 --> 00:58:28.559
what these interviews are saying to

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:29.560
us.

00:58:29.990 --> 00:58:32.210
Ayeka, where are you?

00:58:33.080 --> 00:58:35.462
You've heard my interview and for most of them

00:58:35.540 --> 00:58:38.440
we know, they never gave consent

00:58:38.470 --> 00:58:40.279
that their interview really should be listened

00:58:40.280 --> 00:58:41.389
to or read.

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:44.766
We are presuming

00:58:45.680 --> 00:58:46.699
that prerogative.

00:58:48.380 --> 00:58:50.989
So given that we have taken that course,

00:58:51.470 --> 00:58:52.470
where are you?

00:58:53.790 --> 00:58:56.340
What does this mean to you that you've heard

00:58:56.640 --> 00:58:59.489
what I've told you about my ordeal?

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:02.500
How has your life

00:59:03.160 --> 00:59:04.819
been altered forever?

00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:07.690
Thanks very much.

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:11.160
Thank you, Alan.

00:59:13.040 --> 00:59:15.379
Where-- I noticed that a few people

00:59:15.380 --> 00:59:17.179
are putting in questions.

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:18.979
You should feel free to, to

00:59:19.850 --> 00:59:22.219
add questions to the Q&amp;A button at the bottom

00:59:22.220 --> 00:59:23.989
of your screen or in the chat, and we'll get

00:59:23.990 --> 00:59:26.389
to them after our

00:59:26.390 --> 00:59:28.730
next two presenters

00:59:28.910 --> 00:59:29.910
finish.

00:59:30.980 --> 00:59:33.170
We're now joined by

00:59:33.920 --> 00:59:36.020
Adam Strohm and

00:59:36.350 --> 00:59:38.929
Mindy Pugh, who are both representing

00:59:39.680 --> 00:59:41.621
the Paul V.

00:59:41.898 --> 00:59:43.339
Galvin Library at the Illinois Institute of

00:59:43.340 --> 00:59:44.663
Technology in Chicago, both

00:59:45.860 --> 00:59:47.479
of whom have worked closely with making the

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:49.538
Boder collection readily accessible to the

00:59:49.790 --> 00:59:50.790
research community.

00:59:52.070 --> 00:59:54.860
Adam is the director of University Archives

00:59:54.890 --> 00:59:56.592
and Special Collections

00:59:57.200 --> 00:59:58.279
at the library at IIT.

00:59:59.360 --> 01:00:01.360
And he

01:00:01.850 --> 01:00:04.279
received his MSLIS from the University

01:00:04.280 --> 01:00:06.436
of-- MLIS from the University of Illinois at

01:00:06.620 --> 01:00:07.620
Urbana-Champaign.

01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:10.639
Mindy Pugh is the University archivist and the

01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:12.144
campus historian at the Paul V.

01:00:12.770 --> 01:00:14.485
Galvin Library, previously employed

01:00:15.170 --> 01:00:16.999
as an archivist and a public historian at the

01:00:17.000 --> 01:00:18.289
Chicago History Museum.

01:00:18.920 --> 01:00:20.899
Mindy not only supports research projects

01:00:20.900 --> 01:00:22.969
within the archives, but across campus,

01:00:23.810 --> 01:00:25.835
teaching history in the humanities department.

01:00:26.360 --> 01:00:29.059
And Mindy received a PhD from the University

01:00:29.060 --> 01:00:30.060
of Chicago.

01:00:31.310 --> 01:00:33.639
So I'm going to hand it over to to you,

01:00:33.650 --> 01:00:34.820
Mindy and Adam.

01:00:43.428 --> 01:00:45.709
Hi, I think Mindy is going to talk first about

01:00:45.710 --> 01:00:47.867
the history of the project.

01:00:47.990 --> 01:00:49.656
Mindy, I think you are still, your

01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:51.570
camera still muted there.

01:00:51.900 --> 01:00:52.900
OK.

01:00:52.901 --> 01:00:53.279
Oh, there you are.

01:00:59.585 --> 01:01:00.585
Good. Thank you.

01:01:03.090 --> 01:01:04.529
OK, I'll go on.

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:06.210
OK, thank you, Stephen.

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:08.699
Thank you, Alan,

01:01:09.000 --> 01:01:11.369
for your, your great, great work

01:01:11.370 --> 01:01:13.660
on this. What I'm going to do is,

01:01:14.080 --> 01:01:17.099
is speak about IIT's relationship

01:01:17.100 --> 01:01:19.739
with Dr. Boder and his work

01:01:20.550 --> 01:01:22.289
and put that in context.

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:24.509
I'm very much a historian.

01:01:24.510 --> 01:01:26.069
I do things very chronologically, which I

01:01:26.070 --> 01:01:28.529
think is, you know, kind of a good foundation

01:01:28.530 --> 01:01:30.960
and reference point for people moving forward.

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:35.099
To say very, very briefly, Dr.

01:01:35.100 --> 01:01:37.011
Boder arrived at Lewis Institute, which

01:01:37.590 --> 01:01:40.199
is one of the two predecessor schools of IIT

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:41.940
in 1927

01:01:42.810 --> 01:01:45.419
and remained at Lewis until

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:47.190
it merged with another school,

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:51.060
predecessor school to create IIT in 1940.

01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:54.929
And then he was at IIT until 1952.

01:01:55.470 --> 01:01:58.409
I won't rehearse the

01:01:58.710 --> 01:01:59.990
chronology of the project.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:01.911
I think that's pretty much preknowledge

01:02:02.670 --> 01:02:04.049
for most of you who are attending this

01:02:04.050 --> 01:02:05.050
conference.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:09.329
He retired from IIT in 1952,

01:02:10.890 --> 01:02:13.559
not in a great relational

01:02:13.560 --> 01:02:14.969
posture with the, with IIT.

01:02:16.290 --> 01:02:18.250
I've seen documentation to indicate that

01:02:18.750 --> 01:02:21.840
he left rather dissatisfied,

01:02:22.230 --> 01:02:24.959
perhaps a 10 month, $10.00 a month pension

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:26.530
was part of it.

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:28.680
But after he left IIT in 1952,

01:02:29.730 --> 01:02:30.955
his ties were pretty much

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:34.290
all in the past at that point.

01:02:34.800 --> 01:02:36.209
He went on to have more productive

01:02:36.210 --> 01:02:37.925
relationships obviously after that,

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.849
in 1952 onward with UCLA,

01:02:42.600 --> 01:02:44.364
his continuing relationship with the

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:46.469
Psychology Museum in Akron,

01:02:47.370 --> 01:02:49.590
which dated back to the 1930s,

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:51.000
that continued.

01:02:51.540 --> 01:02:53.639
And of course, then he developed ties in

01:02:53.640 --> 01:02:54.640
Israel as well.

01:02:54.840 --> 01:02:55.840
And those were

01:02:57.300 --> 01:02:59.759
his moving forward, points of reference.

01:03:00.450 --> 01:03:01.675
That said IIT's legacy is

01:03:02.820 --> 01:03:04.800
still very important.

01:03:05.460 --> 01:03:06.979
But for us, it's been a process

01:03:07.980 --> 01:03:09.269
of rediscovery.

01:03:10.020 --> 01:03:12.078
It's not been a continuous connection with

01:03:12.420 --> 01:03:13.743
him either personally or in

01:03:14.730 --> 01:03:16.769
terms of his work since 1952.

01:03:16.770 --> 01:03:19.259
We've had to also rediscover

01:03:19.950 --> 01:03:22.499
Dr. Boder in what he meant and what he means

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:23.500
to this day.

01:03:24.330 --> 01:03:25.996
IIT went through a tough financial

01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:29.009
time in the 1970s and 1980s,

01:03:29.700 --> 01:03:31.829
to the point after the economic downturn of

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:34.349
the early 1990s, there was quite

01:03:34.350 --> 01:03:36.610
an open question as to whether the, whether

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:39.449
IIT would even continue as an institution

01:03:39.780 --> 01:03:41.789
or whether it should move to, say, DuPage

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:42.940
County.

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:44.980
Fortunately, in the mid 1990s,

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:47.789
a number of prominent

01:03:48.450 --> 01:03:51.239
individuals, Bob Galvin of Motorola

01:03:51.240 --> 01:03:53.610
and Bob Pritzker, stepped forward

01:03:54.300 --> 01:03:55.889
to offer matching funds.

01:03:55.890 --> 01:03:57.869
And IIT was basically reborn

01:03:58.290 --> 01:03:59.759
in the mid 1990s.

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:03.029
As part of that rebirth, in that recommitment

01:04:03.030 --> 01:04:04.942
to its south side campus at 35th and

01:04:05.520 --> 01:04:07.889
State and its rediscovery

01:04:07.890 --> 01:04:10.409
of its heritage so that it could truly

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:12.749
anchor and be reborn in its old

01:04:12.750 --> 01:04:15.150
location, it started to,

01:04:15.780 --> 01:04:17.909
as a corporation, take a much more serious

01:04:17.910 --> 01:04:20.250
look at history and the institutional history.

01:04:20.670 --> 01:04:22.949
The university archives, which had gone into a

01:04:22.950 --> 01:04:25.289
period of abeyance during that latter

01:04:25.290 --> 01:04:27.005
part of the crisis years was reborn

01:04:27.960 --> 01:04:30.539
in 1998, and

01:04:30.540 --> 01:04:31.590
Galvin library

01:04:33.060 --> 01:04:34.726
entered into a more dynamic period

01:04:35.730 --> 01:04:38.279
of trying to promote institutional history

01:04:38.280 --> 01:04:40.199
from the late 1990s onward.

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:43.079
I can't speak about IIT without mentioning

01:04:43.080 --> 01:04:44.697
two individuals at Galvin Library

01:04:45.690 --> 01:04:47.258
in that context in the 1990s for

01:04:48.090 --> 01:04:50.429
whom the Boder project owes a great deal.

01:04:51.090 --> 01:04:52.425
The first is Christopher Stewart.

01:04:53.730 --> 01:04:56.060
Christopher Stewart was hired at Galvin

01:04:56.340 --> 01:04:59.009
Library in the mid 1990s to spearhead

01:05:00.300 --> 01:05:02.340
moving the card catalog to digital

01:05:02.880 --> 01:05:05.310
and also to promote Web developments.

01:05:05.370 --> 01:05:06.399
Early efforts to move

01:05:07.710 --> 01:05:09.209
into the electronic era.

01:05:09.990 --> 01:05:12.270
And very closely associated with him was

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:14.192
Kristen Standaert, who's still there.

01:05:14.310 --> 01:05:16.739
Kristen is the associate dean for

01:05:16.740 --> 01:05:17.740
administration.

01:05:18.060 --> 01:05:20.399
Those two spearheaded a lot of

01:05:20.430 --> 01:05:21.851
the forward thinking projects

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:25.616
that would move Galvin Library into the 21st

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:26.740
century.

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:28.600
And

01:05:29.970 --> 01:05:32.130
Christopher and Kristen,

01:05:33.300 --> 01:05:35.639
at a point in the late 1990s when they are

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:38.369
looking for projects to to

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:40.559
put online and to create

01:05:40.800 --> 01:05:43.029
sort of a Web presence for Galvin Library

01:05:43.130 --> 01:05:44.130
and for IIT,

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:47.460
tries some sort of preliminary efforts.

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:49.320
But then they were

01:05:50.370 --> 01:05:52.220
clued into the existence, or were reminded,

01:05:52.800 --> 01:05:55.040
or indeed informed about David Boder

01:05:55.320 --> 01:05:57.476
and his legacy by the psychology department,

01:05:57.709 --> 01:06:00.029
Dr. Ellen Mitchell,

01:06:00.030 --> 01:06:02.429
who is head of the College

01:06:02.430 --> 01:06:04.833
of Psychology at IIT in the late 1990s.

01:06:05.430 --> 01:06:08.159
She had happened upon Galvin Library's

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:09.826
copy of the Boder transcripts from

01:06:10.590 --> 01:06:13.139
the 1950s and they had always

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:13.679
been there.

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:16.650
But she saw it as something of a rediscovery

01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:18.728
in terms of alerting her to this

01:06:19.500 --> 01:06:21.869
important person in the history of her own

01:06:22.170 --> 01:06:23.985
College of Psychology at IIT.

01:06:24.810 --> 01:06:27.269
And she, she

01:06:27.270 --> 01:06:29.960
very quickly liaisoned with Christopher

01:06:29.970 --> 01:06:31.342
Stewart in terms of creating

01:06:33.150 --> 01:06:34.963
a Web presence for these rediscovered

01:06:35.550 --> 01:06:36.550
transcriptions.

01:06:36.810 --> 01:06:39.239
And so this is after

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:41.789
a period of over 40 years

01:06:41.790 --> 01:06:44.111
where IIT basically lost sight of David

01:06:44.280 --> 01:06:45.897
Boder, that there's a rediscovery

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:49.079
of this legacy, and it's an IIT legacy

01:06:49.290 --> 01:06:50.290
in large part.

01:06:51.060 --> 01:06:53.159
That this

01:06:53.430 --> 01:06:55.679
synergy and rivalry to a certain degree

01:06:55.680 --> 01:06:57.493
between the College of Psychology and

01:06:58.290 --> 01:07:00.240
Galvin Library emerges,

01:07:00.900 --> 01:07:02.219
but it has great product.

01:07:03.030 --> 01:07:05.100
In 1998, 1999,

01:07:05.670 --> 01:07:07.190
Kristen Standaert and Christopher Stewart

01:07:07.860 --> 01:07:09.526
negotiate with Library of Congress

01:07:10.380 --> 01:07:11.550
to obtain a

01:07:12.780 --> 01:07:15.509
copy set of the Library of Congress's wires

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:17.639
so that IIT could

01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:20.204
return to itself

01:07:21.030 --> 01:07:23.130
Boder's interviews, audio files.

01:07:23.940 --> 01:07:26.429
And they, I believe, put

01:07:26.670 --> 01:07:28.860
twelve of those audio interviews

01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:31.654
on the initial Voice's site, which I think was

01:07:31.680 --> 01:07:33.039
inaugurated in 1999.

01:07:34.170 --> 01:07:36.749
And then they put up about 40

01:07:36.750 --> 01:07:38.890
to 50 of the transcripts.

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:41.969
Now, this is before OCR character recognition

01:07:41.970 --> 01:07:43.100
was very effective.

01:07:44.070 --> 01:07:47.100
So Stewart and Standaert involved

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:50.039
a large portion of the staff at Galvin library

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:52.680
in reentering those transcripts

01:07:53.040 --> 01:07:54.900
so that they could be put online.

01:07:55.410 --> 01:07:56.782
I hear to this day testimony

01:07:57.720 --> 01:08:00.089
from various of the staffers there who

01:08:00.330 --> 01:08:02.669
survived from that era who said that,

01:08:02.670 --> 01:08:05.039
how interesting, but also how traumatizing it

01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:07.499
was, to have to enter in

01:08:07.500 --> 01:08:10.160
every last word of, from those transcripts.

01:08:11.190 --> 01:08:13.290
The upside of that, it was certainly a

01:08:13.710 --> 01:08:15.719
community all hands on deck

01:08:16.050 --> 01:08:18.499
thing and a real reintroduction

01:08:20.609 --> 01:08:23.369
of the Boder legacy into Galvin library

01:08:23.370 --> 01:08:24.989
through that that whole process.

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.289
Christopher Stewart went on to become library

01:08:28.290 --> 01:08:30.719
dean, and I believe it was 2006,

01:08:30.720 --> 01:08:32.789
just a few months before I came on board from

01:08:32.790 --> 01:08:34.679
the Chicago History Museum, where I had been

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:35.710
for some time.

01:08:36.420 --> 01:08:37.769
And I have to,

01:08:39.120 --> 01:08:41.609
Christopher was very-- is, he's no longer

01:08:41.640 --> 01:08:42.914
at IIT, a very interesting

01:08:44.040 --> 01:08:45.040
individual.

01:08:45.090 --> 01:08:47.369
Christopher Stewart, and again, you cannot

01:08:47.370 --> 01:08:49.909
gainsay his import to all this,

01:08:51.600 --> 01:08:52.679
had a high sense of

01:08:54.030 --> 01:08:55.170
commitment to truth.

01:08:55.950 --> 01:08:58.469
For him, a library is not just a space,

01:08:58.470 --> 01:09:00.675
it's not just a kind of a meeting place, it's

01:09:00.899 --> 01:09:02.663
not just simply the dissemination of

01:09:03.060 --> 01:09:04.890
information. It's a place where

01:09:05.729 --> 01:09:06.954
people are invited to, to

01:09:08.130 --> 01:09:10.239
look at evidence and to verify truth.

01:09:11.250 --> 01:09:12.426
He had very much a sense

01:09:13.859 --> 01:09:15.084
that truth is a contested

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:18.177
zone. And of course, we nowadays,

01:09:19.439 --> 01:09:20.439
in the era of

01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:24.136
Trumpism, we tend to see that now,

01:09:24.840 --> 01:09:25.889
we recognize that.

01:09:25.899 --> 01:09:28.259
But Christopher has a sort of foreboding of

01:09:28.260 --> 01:09:29.260
that.

01:09:29.670 --> 01:09:31.581
History didn't end with the fall of the

01:09:32.069 --> 01:09:33.779
Berlin Wall in 1989.

01:09:34.080 --> 01:09:36.659
We all kind of hoped it would, that

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:39.119
things would usher

01:09:39.120 --> 01:09:41.639
in a period of amity between East and West

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:43.680
and that those rivalries would be gone.

01:09:44.250 --> 01:09:46.559
But unfortunately, the Yugoslav civil war in

01:09:46.560 --> 01:09:49.380
the 1990s, 9/11 in 2001,

01:09:50.100 --> 01:09:52.260
and the hard line

01:09:52.830 --> 01:09:55.439
Iranian leadership in 2003,

01:09:55.630 --> 01:09:58.259
4, 5, under President Ahmadinejad

01:09:58.620 --> 01:10:00.384
really revealed that truth was still

01:10:00.960 --> 01:10:02.850
going to be contested, that

01:10:03.270 --> 01:10:05.579
narratives could still be worked and

01:10:05.580 --> 01:10:06.569
shifted.

01:10:06.570 --> 01:10:08.939
And Christopher Stewart was very determined

01:10:09.150 --> 01:10:10.799
to make sure that people could judge for

01:10:10.800 --> 01:10:11.789
themselves.

01:10:11.790 --> 01:10:13.859
Read the transcripts, hear all of the audio

01:10:13.860 --> 01:10:15.779
interviews, have all of that out there.

01:10:16.110 --> 01:10:18.390
And once he became dean in 2006, he,

01:10:18.800 --> 01:10:20.909
he moved that project forward from just a

01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:22.919
small sampling of materials to as much of

01:10:23.280 --> 01:10:25.350
the audio files as he could put online,

01:10:25.710 --> 01:10:27.149
and as many of the, and, all of the

01:10:27.150 --> 01:10:29.090
transcripts and translation, and in

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:31.130
transcription that he could, could muster.

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:34.199
And so the second iteration of the

01:10:35.070 --> 01:10:37.319
site went up in 2009.

01:10:37.470 --> 01:10:40.019
And yes, there was something of a real

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:41.020
sort of...again,

01:10:43.500 --> 01:10:46.439
as something of a real philosophic commitment

01:10:46.440 --> 01:10:48.498
that Christopher Stewart had to that which

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:50.596
was very exciting to work with, in

01:10:51.240 --> 01:10:52.240
terms of that.

01:10:52.530 --> 01:10:54.329
For me professionally, it was very fulfilling

01:10:54.330 --> 01:10:56.094
because I came with a history degree

01:10:56.910 --> 01:10:58.576
in European studies, not just U.S.

01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:00.711
studies, and to be able to support that

01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:02.290
effort.

01:11:04.820 --> 01:11:07.249
Again, that notion of contested space

01:11:08.120 --> 01:11:09.443
that we we live in, as John

01:11:10.640 --> 01:11:12.649
Kennedy said in 1961,

01:11:13.070 --> 01:11:14.644
a period of hard and bitter peace.

01:11:14.645 --> 01:11:16.752
We still are in a period of hard and bitter

01:11:17.120 --> 01:11:18.620
peace, where

01:11:19.880 --> 01:11:21.693
we need to stand up against demagogic

01:11:22.220 --> 01:11:24.033
populism and we need to really commit

01:11:24.800 --> 01:11:26.960
ourselves to robust

01:11:27.170 --> 01:11:28.640
platforms where evidence is at

01:11:29.630 --> 01:11:31.970
the foundation, so that

01:11:32.450 --> 01:11:34.779
at least people of, of, of

01:11:34.820 --> 01:11:36.045
good, good, good will and

01:11:37.220 --> 01:11:39.619
a commitment to truth as we understand it in

01:11:39.620 --> 01:11:42.259
academe could strengthen

01:11:42.260 --> 01:11:44.089
themselves and carry forward

01:11:44.720 --> 01:11:47.539
Dr. Boder's message, which was,

01:11:48.110 --> 01:11:50.209
and still is, his legacy lives,

01:11:50.960 --> 01:11:53.090
a commitment to the truth.

01:11:53.330 --> 01:11:56.029
Let history and let the world be a judge

01:11:56.030 --> 01:11:57.794
of his work and let it be a judge of

01:11:58.460 --> 01:12:00.799
what actually did, did occur.

01:12:01.110 --> 01:12:03.439
So, I'm going to turn it over

01:12:03.440 --> 01:12:05.869
to Adam. But that's, that's my contribution

01:12:05.870 --> 01:12:06.870
on that.

01:12:10.040 --> 01:12:11.040
Thank you, Mindy.

01:12:11.960 --> 01:12:14.089
So I'm going to talk a little bit

01:12:14.750 --> 01:12:17.269
about the current state of

01:12:17.270 --> 01:12:19.249
the Voices of the Holocaust project.

01:12:20.690 --> 01:12:22.209
I will give you a kind of sneak

01:12:23.300 --> 01:12:24.574
preview of the new version

01:12:25.670 --> 01:12:27.924
of the site that we're working on now and kind

01:12:28.040 --> 01:12:30.590
of discuss what we're hoping

01:12:30.860 --> 01:12:31.938
this new move can kind

01:12:33.170 --> 01:12:35.749
of bring to the material

01:12:35.750 --> 01:12:38.120
and people's experiences with it.

01:12:39.890 --> 01:12:41.213
Let me just share my screen

01:12:42.710 --> 01:12:43.710
here.

01:12:46.440 --> 01:12:49.080
OK, so this

01:12:49.200 --> 01:12:51.509
is the Voices of the Holocaust

01:12:51.510 --> 01:12:53.909
site, as Mindy mentioned, that

01:12:53.910 --> 01:12:54.910
was put

01:12:56.250 --> 01:12:57.656
up in 2009.

01:12:58.950 --> 01:13:01.800
And this was the Voices of the Holocaust

01:13:01.830 --> 01:13:04.295
as it existed until very recently.

01:13:06.240 --> 01:13:08.549
The site was, of

01:13:08.550 --> 01:13:11.089
course, generated a lot of traffic.

01:13:11.670 --> 01:13:14.039
We were constantly

01:13:14.040 --> 01:13:16.330
chatting with folks about it, interested

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:18.839
Holocaust scholars, high school students,

01:13:19.890 --> 01:13:20.890
you name it.

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:23.639
So it was a very popular site and we knew

01:13:23.640 --> 01:13:26.489
that. But we also knew that it was beginning,

01:13:26.490 --> 01:13:28.169
technology-wise to get a little long in the

01:13:28.170 --> 01:13:29.170
tooth.

01:13:29.460 --> 01:13:31.829
And the point at which we knew we

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:34.680
needed to make a move was when

01:13:34.980 --> 01:13:36.744
Web browsers started disabling Flash

01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:38.960
technology by default.

01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:41.073
This meant that essentially

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:45.449
to get any of the interviews to play

01:13:45.450 --> 01:13:48.090
on a modern Web browser,

01:13:48.420 --> 01:13:50.527
you had to know where to go to tell it that

01:13:50.760 --> 01:13:51.989
it was OK to use Flash.

01:13:52.020 --> 01:13:54.380
So I, I had an email copying,

01:13:54.900 --> 01:13:56.579
an email that I copied and pasted, I don't

01:13:56.580 --> 01:13:58.919
know how many times explaining to people

01:13:58.920 --> 01:13:59.920
how to do this.

01:14:00.120 --> 01:14:01.889
And it became clear that this, this just

01:14:01.890 --> 01:14:02.890
wasn't tenable.

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:06.425
We also knew that the Text Encoding

01:14:06.990 --> 01:14:09.244
Initiative, TEI format that was used to encode

01:14:09.600 --> 01:14:11.610
the interview translations and transcripts

01:14:12.270 --> 01:14:14.699
wasn't quite, it was, it did

01:14:14.700 --> 01:14:16.699
its job really, really well.

01:14:16.710 --> 01:14:19.469
But it wasn't as interoperable with

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:22.109
modern Web technologies in terms

01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:23.874
of video playback and audio and text

01:14:24.060 --> 01:14:25.060
synchronizing.

01:14:25.680 --> 01:14:27.591
And we were kind of kicking around some

01:14:28.050 --> 01:14:30.780
ideas. We had, a few of my colleagues

01:14:31.110 --> 01:14:33.569
were working on some demos

01:14:33.570 --> 01:14:34.679
of some things that might work.

01:14:34.680 --> 01:14:36.720
And that's when Stephen approached us

01:14:37.080 --> 01:14:39.629
about the idea of adding the

01:14:39.900 --> 01:14:42.239
Boder interviews into

01:14:42.240 --> 01:14:44.053
the Aviary platform so they could sit

01:14:44.790 --> 01:14:47.159
alongside the Fortunoff Archive interviews.

01:14:47.160 --> 01:14:49.200
And not only

01:14:49.500 --> 01:14:52.079
was the idea of bringing these all together

01:14:52.620 --> 01:14:53.620
a great idea, but

01:14:54.930 --> 01:14:57.810
we realized after a little bit of exploration

01:14:58.620 --> 01:15:00.629
that the Aviary platform

01:15:01.470 --> 01:15:03.380
could serve as a,

01:15:04.650 --> 01:15:06.989
a way to kind of service

01:15:06.990 --> 01:15:09.479
the techno--technological underpinning of

01:15:09.630 --> 01:15:11.500
the new website.

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:14.729
So this is the interviews as they look in

01:15:14.730 --> 01:15:15.730
Aviary.

01:15:17.900 --> 01:15:20.390
And I'm kind of playing around within

01:15:20.690 --> 01:15:22.640
the Illinois Institute of Technology

01:15:23.090 --> 01:15:25.579
collection here, but as Stephen mentioned,

01:15:26.540 --> 01:15:28.879
these interviews will come up in a search

01:15:29.210 --> 01:15:30.949
right alongside the Fortunoff Archive

01:15:30.950 --> 01:15:32.469
interviews. So, you know, there

01:15:33.350 --> 01:15:35.960
is a, there's a real

01:15:36.110 --> 01:15:38.266
kind of connection being made here that just

01:15:38.600 --> 01:15:40.489
wasn't there before, for the Boder interviews.

01:15:44.350 --> 01:15:45.350
So if we look for

01:15:46.930 --> 01:15:48.008
the aforementioned Mr.

01:15:49.240 --> 01:15:50.949
Silberbard, you'll see that.

01:15:53.230 --> 01:15:55.435
Maybe, maybe his Fortunoff interview, well, I

01:15:55.690 --> 01:15:57.129
should have done this search ahead of time,

01:15:57.130 --> 01:15:59.286
but I decided to improvise based on previous

01:15:59.680 --> 01:16:00.680
content.

01:16:00.850 --> 01:16:03.610
But regardless, you can see here

01:16:04.360 --> 01:16:06.069
that interviews are popping up next to each

01:16:06.070 --> 01:16:08.499
other. And this is, to my knowledge,

01:16:08.500 --> 01:16:10.329
the first time that

01:16:11.200 --> 01:16:13.240
the Boder interviews, that kind of a

01:16:13.840 --> 01:16:16.149
word by word level have been kind

01:16:16.150 --> 01:16:17.380
of taken out of their silo.

01:16:19.540 --> 01:16:21.939
So then I'll give you a brief demo

01:16:21.940 --> 01:16:24.399
of where we're at now, starting with

01:16:24.400 --> 01:16:26.769
the, the apology that this is

01:16:27.160 --> 01:16:29.229
visually, this is very bare bones at this

01:16:29.230 --> 01:16:31.329
point, this site. We've, most of the work

01:16:31.330 --> 01:16:33.729
we've undertaken to this point is of more of

01:16:33.730 --> 01:16:34.730
like a technological

01:16:36.190 --> 01:16:38.950
and data migration

01:16:39.400 --> 01:16:41.829
efforts. We have not done a whole lot in terms

01:16:41.830 --> 01:16:42.830
of design.

01:16:43.060 --> 01:16:45.399
So take that in mind

01:16:45.400 --> 01:16:47.829
as I, as I kind of drive through here.

01:16:48.700 --> 01:16:51.400
This site is built in Drupal

01:16:51.580 --> 01:16:53.250
using software called Islandora.

01:16:53.830 --> 01:16:55.689
These are two platforms that

01:16:56.200 --> 01:16:58.249
we were already working with and committed to,

01:16:58.540 --> 01:17:00.850
so it became a very natural way to do this.

01:17:01.840 --> 01:17:04.479
And we migrated, we tried to migrate

01:17:04.510 --> 01:17:07.119
every little bit of data that we had from

01:17:07.120 --> 01:17:08.760
the previous site over to this one.

01:17:09.370 --> 01:17:11.109
There are a handful of things we're still

01:17:11.110 --> 01:17:12.399
working on integrating.

01:17:13.860 --> 01:17:16.050
But that meant a lot of,

01:17:16.380 --> 01:17:18.749
a lot of remediation that had to happen

01:17:18.750 --> 01:17:20.879
by hand, especially because Aviary

01:17:21.240 --> 01:17:22.640
wanted data in some ways--formats.

01:17:24.520 --> 01:17:26.500
Islandora wanted it in others,

01:17:26.830 --> 01:17:29.139
and so a good bit of my time

01:17:29.140 --> 01:17:30.759
over the past six to eight months has been

01:17:30.760 --> 01:17:33.189
spent kind of going hand by

01:17:33.190 --> 01:17:35.109
hand through these interviews at times.

01:17:35.110 --> 01:17:37.204
And I will echo what Mindy said about it,

01:17:37.480 --> 01:17:39.999
it's been very gratifying and

01:17:40.540 --> 01:17:42.879
intellectually stimulating work at times, but

01:17:42.880 --> 01:17:45.399
not, not always the easiest

01:17:45.400 --> 01:17:47.066
thing to spend, spend my day going

01:17:47.920 --> 01:17:49.620
through, especially during a pandemic.

01:17:51.410 --> 01:17:53.419
So there were some interesting times this

01:17:53.600 --> 01:17:54.600
summer for me.

01:17:56.300 --> 01:17:57.525
So I want to just briefly

01:17:58.670 --> 01:18:01.279
kind of make a little run through

01:18:01.280 --> 01:18:03.769
things here and talk about some

01:18:03.770 --> 01:18:05.560
of what this site will provide that the older

01:18:05.570 --> 01:18:06.570
site didn't.

01:18:08.060 --> 01:18:09.922
So the the search and browse interface

01:18:10.670 --> 01:18:12.065
is a bit more,

01:18:13.130 --> 01:18:14.845
a bit more in line with what modern

01:18:15.590 --> 01:18:17.479
Internet users are going to look for,

01:18:17.720 --> 01:18:18.790
including things like faceting.

01:18:19.350 --> 01:18:21.709
So I can take the interviews and say,

01:18:21.710 --> 01:18:23.467
well, I only want people in their twenties.

01:18:25.640 --> 01:18:27.949
And, you know, people

01:18:27.950 --> 01:18:29.439
in their twenties that were born in Greece,

01:18:30.050 --> 01:18:31.790
and so it's very easy to kind of

01:18:33.020 --> 01:18:34.245
narrow in on a particular

01:18:35.510 --> 01:18:37.709
interview or a particular set of interviews.

01:18:39.560 --> 01:18:42.269
We, of course, have searching by terms.

01:18:42.290 --> 01:18:43.858
And so if I'm looking for any of

01:18:44.660 --> 01:18:46.909
the interviewees who, say, worked in a factory

01:18:47.420 --> 01:18:49.579
or talk about work in factories,

01:18:50.210 --> 01:18:52.669
I can then again do this, this same sort

01:18:52.670 --> 01:18:54.979
of narrowing down to

01:18:54.980 --> 01:18:56.199
find what I'm looking for.

01:18:56.510 --> 01:18:58.715
We're working on this knowing that some folks

01:18:58.970 --> 01:19:00.529
are going to be coming, just kind of

01:19:00.530 --> 01:19:02.750
interested in the idea of this project

01:19:03.200 --> 01:19:05.062
and not really looking for anything in

01:19:05.090 --> 01:19:07.129
particular. But there will definitely be

01:19:07.130 --> 01:19:09.619
scholars who are

01:19:09.620 --> 01:19:11.286
looking to find some very specific

01:19:12.260 --> 01:19:13.579
things in these interviews.

01:19:13.580 --> 01:19:15.239
And we want to try to allow for both.

01:19:17.690 --> 01:19:20.119
So within the interviews here, you can

01:19:20.120 --> 01:19:22.999
see that it embeds a player window from

01:19:23.570 --> 01:19:25.580
Aviary into the site here.

01:19:26.330 --> 01:19:28.729
And we have and you'll

01:19:28.730 --> 01:19:30.499
see that much of the search that I did there

01:19:30.500 --> 01:19:31.729
is represented here.

01:19:31.730 --> 01:19:33.709
And I can actually jump directly,

01:19:34.580 --> 01:19:35.580
directly to those.

01:19:35.610 --> 01:19:37.459
So we're trying to make it as easy as possible

01:19:37.460 --> 01:19:39.159
for people to get to what they want to hear.

01:19:40.670 --> 01:19:41.670
And then I can immediately

01:19:44.900 --> 01:19:47.056
jump to this spot in the audio and I'll just

01:19:47.060 --> 01:19:48.761
let this interview play for just a moment.

01:19:49.969 --> 01:19:51.427
--the office maybe I wouldn't be alive anymore

01:19:54.080 --> 01:19:55.235
because the factory work would be too difficult
for me.

01:19:56.693 --> 01:19:58.023
Yes. Did they pay anything for the work?

01:19:58.024 --> 01:19:58.192
Nothing.

01:20:01.002 --> 01:20:02.193
Well, when you needed some things, soap

01:20:03.060 --> 01:20:04.060
or...did you have toothpaste?

01:20:06.640 --> 01:20:08.039
Oh. We didn't get anything

01:20:08.980 --> 01:20:10.288
from them. Just one little piece of soap

01:20:11.350 --> 01:20:13.613
and we had to wash ourselves only with water.

01:20:15.660 --> 01:20:16.660
You washed yourself only with water.

01:20:17.410 --> 01:20:19.442
And oh yes, we got one piece of

01:20:20.050 --> 01:20:21.978
soap, but that wasn't enough for the whole

01:20:21.979 --> 01:20:22.979
month.

01:20:24.010 --> 01:20:25.159
And how did you handle your laundry?

01:20:28.390 --> 01:20:29.410
Or did you wash you clothing?

01:20:32.990 --> 01:20:35.010
Everybody knows that in camp wasn't so clean.

01:20:37.220 --> 01:20:39.890
So that's an example of how,

01:20:40.210 --> 01:20:42.379
how someone looking for a very specific thing

01:20:42.380 --> 01:20:44.487
can kind of, hopefully access it as quickly

01:20:44.960 --> 01:20:45.960
as possible.

01:20:46.700 --> 01:20:48.979
Another really important part of this project

01:20:49.160 --> 01:20:51.380
that I think has

01:20:51.650 --> 01:20:54.289
something to do with the

01:20:55.040 --> 01:20:57.650
kind of the idea that

01:20:58.430 --> 01:21:00.769
Stephen was talking about earlier, and Alan

01:21:00.770 --> 01:21:02.719
and Mindy have all mentioned this, this idea

01:21:02.720 --> 01:21:05.000
of connecting, connecting this work.

01:21:06.410 --> 01:21:09.200
And one of the things that is,

01:21:09.290 --> 01:21:11.397
has been fairly big in kind of Web projects

01:21:11.960 --> 01:21:14.029
of this sort over the past, I don't know,

01:21:14.030 --> 01:21:16.220
decade or so is this idea of linked open data

01:21:16.880 --> 01:21:18.150
and this idea that

01:21:20.270 --> 01:21:22.759
all of these data points within

01:21:22.760 --> 01:21:25.339
these interviews are things

01:21:25.340 --> 01:21:27.055
that other people in other projects

01:21:27.680 --> 01:21:29.580
and other places are also referencing.

01:21:30.110 --> 01:21:32.569
And so one thing that we tried really hard

01:21:32.570 --> 01:21:34.824
to do is to build on already, I don't know how

01:21:34.880 --> 01:21:37.189
many hours of work done kind of laying

01:21:37.250 --> 01:21:39.319
that, laying the groundwork for this in the

01:21:39.320 --> 01:21:40.320
initial site.

01:21:40.790 --> 01:21:42.979
But we tried to as much as we could.

01:21:42.980 --> 01:21:45.109
And there are still a handful that I'm playing

01:21:45.110 --> 01:21:47.300
with a little bit here, link,

01:21:47.960 --> 01:21:50.629
link out records for any camps, people,

01:21:50.630 --> 01:21:53.269
or places to online authorities.

01:21:54.230 --> 01:21:55.230
And to try to do

01:21:56.600 --> 01:21:58.168
things like represent, represent

01:21:59.390 --> 01:22:01.759
which camps were subsidiaries of

01:22:01.760 --> 01:22:03.328
others, simple things like which

01:22:04.190 --> 01:22:06.229
cities are in which country, and you can now

01:22:06.230 --> 01:22:08.629
kind of balance between a city, look at every

01:22:08.630 --> 01:22:10.786
city mentioned in that country and jump over

01:22:10.970 --> 01:22:11.970
to those.

01:22:12.620 --> 01:22:14.989
And then we, we also have a way to represent,

01:22:15.290 --> 01:22:16.939
you know, here is every interview

01:22:17.750 --> 01:22:20.150
that Gross-Rosen is mentioned.

01:22:22.120 --> 01:22:23.492
So there is, there is also a

01:22:24.640 --> 01:22:26.739
very uninteresting kind of

01:22:27.070 --> 01:22:29.229
underpinning to this that my colleague Tim

01:22:29.230 --> 01:22:31.479
Flohr is going to work on, that will allow

01:22:31.480 --> 01:22:33.440
people to kind of access this data under

01:22:33.850 --> 01:22:36.101
the hood, let's say, and, you know

01:22:37.950 --> 01:22:40.319
that way, if, you know one

01:22:40.320 --> 01:22:42.182
of the big things in, in projects like

01:22:42.810 --> 01:22:43.937
this over the past five

01:22:45.210 --> 01:22:47.317
to ten years has been people building these

01:22:47.340 --> 01:22:49.104
portals that give you kind of access

01:22:50.100 --> 01:22:51.717
across a spectrum of institutions

01:22:52.410 --> 01:22:53.760
or projects.

01:22:54.450 --> 01:22:56.263
And this will also, the work that Tim

01:22:56.880 --> 01:22:58.739
is doing is also going to make that possible

01:22:58.740 --> 01:23:00.994
for these Boder interviews, that we'll be able

01:23:01.140 --> 01:23:02.753
to hand someone a, you know, a

01:23:04.880 --> 01:23:07.369
figurative bucket of data and have them

01:23:07.370 --> 01:23:08.840
loaded into their system, that

01:23:09.800 --> 01:23:11.368
will hopefully allow us to point

01:23:12.350 --> 01:23:14.689
people to these interviews and

01:23:14.690 --> 01:23:16.650
to have people find them without already

01:23:17.060 --> 01:23:18.208
having to know they exist.

01:23:18.650 --> 01:23:20.989
Because up until now, a lot, in

01:23:20.990 --> 01:23:22.789
a lot of ways you would have to know that the

01:23:22.790 --> 01:23:24.699
Boder interviews existed to find them.

01:23:24.710 --> 01:23:26.330
And it is our goal to

01:23:27.440 --> 01:23:29.170
make that not so necessary.

01:23:32.210 --> 01:23:34.729
One other thing I wanted to quickly point out,

01:23:35.890 --> 01:23:38.479
you know, the

01:23:38.480 --> 01:23:40.342
work that Alan and others have done to

01:23:41.180 --> 01:23:43.010
contextualize Boder's work

01:23:43.910 --> 01:23:46.519
and to tell his story is,

01:23:46.730 --> 01:23:48.592
you know, I will speak for myself, but

01:23:49.100 --> 01:23:51.079
I think I can speak for us as an institution.

01:23:51.090 --> 01:23:52.669
It's, you know, it's not something that we

01:23:52.670 --> 01:23:54.777
would be able to do. And we've, we've tried

01:23:55.340 --> 01:23:56.340
to take a just the

01:23:57.680 --> 01:23:59.989
facts sort of approach to this

01:23:59.990 --> 01:24:02.479
and allow others to kind of expand on

01:24:02.720 --> 01:24:05.158
that story where they have the expertise

01:24:05.330 --> 01:24:06.330
to do so. But we, we

01:24:07.640 --> 01:24:10.250
have been able to include things like

01:24:10.370 --> 01:24:12.439
Boder's annotations that were present in his

01:24:12.440 --> 01:24:14.869
transcript, as well as for

01:24:14.870 --> 01:24:17.689
selected interviews, we have scholarly

01:24:17.720 --> 01:24:20.299
annotation and commentary and

01:24:20.300 --> 01:24:21.700
we've included that as well.

01:24:22.220 --> 01:24:23.886
But for the most part, we have not

01:24:24.920 --> 01:24:27.739
done any additional sort of contextualization

01:24:28.460 --> 01:24:31.069
outside of the kind of linking to

01:24:31.070 --> 01:24:32.829
authorities and that sort of thing.

01:24:33.770 --> 01:24:35.749
It's something we're not averse to.

01:24:35.750 --> 01:24:37.880
But we did not feel that we were, we were in

01:24:38.150 --> 01:24:40.022
in a situation where we can do that now.

01:24:41.780 --> 01:24:43.909
In terms of what's left to do on the site,

01:24:44.150 --> 01:24:46.009
there's still a little bit of research work I

01:24:46.010 --> 01:24:47.010
have to do on town

01:24:48.380 --> 01:24:50.291
names and things like that, that people

01:24:50.750 --> 01:24:52.465
said and maybe Boder perhaps didn't

01:24:53.210 --> 01:24:54.379
transcribe correctly.

01:24:54.380 --> 01:24:56.180
And so for decades it's been a little,

01:24:57.170 --> 01:24:58.459
a little foggy.

01:24:58.460 --> 01:25:00.799
I don't, I don't claim to say that I will

01:25:00.800 --> 01:25:03.199
be the one to figure it out, but trying

01:25:03.200 --> 01:25:04.200
to do my best.

01:25:04.760 --> 01:25:06.829
Obviously, there's some design and aesthetic

01:25:06.830 --> 01:25:07.909
work to be done here.

01:25:09.560 --> 01:25:11.510
And then a lot of the work under,

01:25:12.290 --> 01:25:14.029
kind of beneath the surface of this that's

01:25:14.030 --> 01:25:16.088
going to make it really interoperable with

01:25:16.130 --> 01:25:17.159
other, other portals,

01:25:18.620 --> 01:25:19.620
other projects.

01:25:19.820 --> 01:25:21.509
That's work that's ongoing as well.

01:25:22.400 --> 01:25:24.560
I wish I could say that we had a great

01:25:25.430 --> 01:25:27.999
date set as kind of the relaunch

01:25:28.000 --> 01:25:29.839
for the Voices of the Holocaust project.

01:25:29.840 --> 01:25:32.030
But to date, we don't.

01:25:32.810 --> 01:25:34.820
This, unfortunately, is not anyones

01:25:36.170 --> 01:25:38.359
full time project right now.

01:25:38.360 --> 01:25:39.781
And as I think anyone, anyone

01:25:40.970 --> 01:25:42.636
can attest to over the last six to

01:25:43.280 --> 01:25:45.319
eight months, it's been, it's been difficult,

01:25:45.320 --> 01:25:47.140
but we're hoping early 2021

01:25:47.630 --> 01:25:49.340
the site will be ready to go.

01:25:50.210 --> 01:25:51.484
And until then, if you, if

01:25:52.580 --> 01:25:53.580
you navigate to voices.iit.edu,

01:25:56.930 --> 01:25:59.269
it will, it will bring you to our

01:25:59.270 --> 01:26:00.936
collection in the Aviary platform.

01:26:01.310 --> 01:26:03.590
So the interviews are still accessible.

01:26:04.520 --> 01:26:06.890
All of the annotations and the

01:26:07.100 --> 01:26:09.354
metadata for the interviews, the descriptions,

01:26:09.680 --> 01:26:11.269
the commentary, it's all there.

01:26:12.770 --> 01:26:14.877
But hopefully within the next few months we

01:26:15.170 --> 01:26:17.424
will have the site done and be able to present

01:26:17.510 --> 01:26:19.666
it in the format that we think makes it most

01:26:20.000 --> 01:26:22.589
usable and also,

01:26:24.160 --> 01:26:26.619
you know, hopefully most

01:26:26.620 --> 01:26:28.154
enlightening to our users.

01:26:29.210 --> 01:26:30.210
And I think that's it.

01:26:30.640 --> 01:26:31.640
Thanks.

01:26:33.180 --> 01:26:35.310
Thanks, Adam, and thanks indeed.

01:26:35.330 --> 01:26:36.819
The site looks, that's the first time I've

01:26:36.820 --> 01:26:39.010
seen the site with the Aviary embeds,

01:26:39.130 --> 01:26:40.769
and it looks very good.

01:26:40.780 --> 01:26:42.100
It's very exciting to see that.

01:26:42.900 --> 01:26:45.519
OK, we have a few questions

01:26:45.520 --> 01:26:46.539
in the Q&amp;A

01:26:48.040 --> 01:26:49.755
tab here, so I'm going to just read

01:26:50.380 --> 01:26:51.129
them off.

01:26:51.130 --> 01:26:52.196
For Alan, this

01:26:55.000 --> 01:26:56.197
one's from Shira Schmitt.

01:26:57.970 --> 01:27:00.126
Does the religious or non-religious identity

01:27:00.640 --> 01:27:02.439
of an interviewer make a difference?

01:27:02.890 --> 01:27:05.740
If so, how does it impact on the interview?

01:27:12.850 --> 01:27:14.260
Thanks for the question.

01:27:17.300 --> 01:27:19.970
Boder was a

01:27:20.060 --> 01:27:22.399
traditional Eastern

01:27:22.400 --> 01:27:24.949
European Jew, so it was very

01:27:24.950 --> 01:27:26.960
important that he could

01:27:27.410 --> 01:27:28.797
actively speak

01:27:29.960 --> 01:27:30.989
six languages and had

01:27:32.510 --> 01:27:34.030
passing knowledge of three others.

01:27:36.990 --> 01:27:38.879
He wasn't a,

01:27:41.490 --> 01:27:42.715
he wasn't a religious Jew

01:27:44.190 --> 01:27:46.299
in the conventional sense.

01:27:46.920 --> 01:27:49.562
So when he interviewed

01:27:52.330 --> 01:27:53.369
those who were,

01:27:55.080 --> 01:27:56.690
which took place the Henonville

01:27:57.570 --> 01:27:59.879
interviews and other places,

01:28:01.470 --> 01:28:03.239
he tried to cope.

01:28:06.060 --> 01:28:08.340
And so

01:28:08.410 --> 01:28:11.300
there is a gap, it makes a difference,

01:28:11.850 --> 01:28:12.850
but what we

01:28:14.160 --> 01:28:15.540
have is his

01:28:16.740 --> 01:28:17.769
eliciting a chronicle

01:28:19.260 --> 01:28:21.204
and commentary from the interviewers,

01:28:22.080 --> 01:28:23.640
religious or not,

01:28:24.450 --> 01:28:27.280
not conventionally religious.

01:28:27.750 --> 01:28:30.720
And so he,

01:28:31.590 --> 01:28:32.850
he did what he could.

01:28:33.690 --> 01:28:35.819
I don't want to say more than that generally.

01:28:36.690 --> 01:28:38.609
Stay, I'll stay within

01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:40.600
the Boder interviews.

01:28:42.020 --> 01:28:43.294
OK, another question, this

01:28:44.390 --> 01:28:45.390
one from Joanne Rudof.

01:28:45.620 --> 01:28:47.139
Why do you think it's desirable

01:28:48.050 --> 01:28:50.239
to popularize David Boder's work,

01:28:50.600 --> 01:28:52.939
since such popularization has the potential to

01:28:52.940 --> 01:28:54.680
be more harmful than helpful?

01:28:59.080 --> 01:29:00.080
That's a great question, Joanne.

01:29:01.530 --> 01:29:02.530
Thanks.

01:29:08.770 --> 01:29:09.770
I think that...popularization

01:29:18.320 --> 01:29:19.540
brings a,

01:29:23.080 --> 01:29:25.180
brings knowledge and awareness.

01:29:27.220 --> 01:29:29.650
And can

01:29:30.990 --> 01:29:33.479
already plant the seed of an idea that

01:29:33.480 --> 01:29:34.480
someone

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:37.305
conceived of.

01:29:39.210 --> 01:29:40.210
Taking such steps

01:29:41.880 --> 01:29:44.249
to an expedition to

01:29:44.250 --> 01:29:46.619
listen to people that he had no connection

01:29:46.620 --> 01:29:48.237
with, so I think that can be very

01:29:49.140 --> 01:29:51.510
inspiring for

01:29:52.140 --> 01:29:54.449
those in their daily life

01:29:55.200 --> 01:29:58.170
as well as for, and

01:29:58.470 --> 01:29:59.790
for academics.

01:30:00.270 --> 01:30:02.745
So I think Boder's initiative was so

01:30:05.570 --> 01:30:08.269
inspired and

01:30:09.350 --> 01:30:11.810
resourceful, that

01:30:12.470 --> 01:30:15.079
it has the possibility

01:30:15.590 --> 01:30:17.720
of bringing that inspiration

01:30:18.080 --> 01:30:19.080
more generally.

01:30:19.160 --> 01:30:21.979
So that's why I think it's important.

01:30:25.270 --> 01:30:26.270
OK.

01:30:27.130 --> 01:30:28.779
Another question from Oren Stier,

01:30:29.800 --> 01:30:31.711
he is intrigued by the notion of public

01:30:31.960 --> 01:30:34.479
listening as something that you, if

01:30:34.480 --> 01:30:36.440
he understood correctly, you spoke about

01:30:36.460 --> 01:30:38.580
public listening in your presentation,

01:30:38.830 --> 01:30:40.899
what might public listening look like in

01:30:40.900 --> 01:30:43.154
practice, both academically and more generally

01:30:43.840 --> 01:30:44.840
today?

01:30:47.590 --> 01:30:49.990
Thanks, Oren, for that

01:30:51.250 --> 01:30:52.579
searching question.

01:30:53.060 --> 01:30:55.087
So, just

01:30:55.450 --> 01:30:57.130
beginning thoughts on that

01:30:59.050 --> 01:31:01.209
and that notion, and look forward to

01:31:01.960 --> 01:31:04.359
the conversing with you

01:31:04.360 --> 01:31:05.970
about it at greater length.

01:31:06.520 --> 01:31:08.829
So I think that the focus

01:31:08.920 --> 01:31:11.470
is often, as I mentioned on public speaking,

01:31:12.100 --> 01:31:14.868
that through one's education, one feels

01:31:16.710 --> 01:31:17.710
pressured

01:31:19.050 --> 01:31:20.870
to speak well,

01:31:21.450 --> 01:31:23.100
express oneself well,

01:31:23.760 --> 01:31:24.985
and that takes,

01:31:26.580 --> 01:31:28.019
that takes precedence.

01:31:29.280 --> 01:31:31.949
And listening can

01:31:31.950 --> 01:31:34.409
also bring a very, very

01:31:34.410 --> 01:31:36.899
great results, as I think

01:31:36.900 --> 01:31:39.260
Boder's interviews

01:31:39.360 --> 01:31:42.240
show, as the Fortunoff Archive

01:31:43.020 --> 01:31:44.020
display.

01:31:44.670 --> 01:31:46.439
So public listening?

01:31:46.510 --> 01:31:49.140
I was thinking of in a sense that

01:31:49.620 --> 01:31:52.319
he was not speaking

01:31:52.320 --> 01:31:54.899
about listening in terms

01:31:54.900 --> 01:31:57.989
of a private activity

01:31:57.990 --> 01:31:59.069
that one does

01:32:01.350 --> 01:32:03.810
on the telephone or with a friend,

01:32:04.980 --> 01:32:08.100
but rather in a way, in a classroom,

01:32:08.430 --> 01:32:11.130
in a lecture, in

01:32:11.880 --> 01:32:14.369
seeking to elicit

01:32:15.930 --> 01:32:18.359
information and a chronicle

01:32:18.360 --> 01:32:21.149
from those who have gone through difficult

01:32:21.570 --> 01:32:22.950
experiences.

01:32:24.060 --> 01:32:26.670
So a public,

01:32:26.970 --> 01:32:29.760
public listening would have

01:32:30.530 --> 01:32:33.209
that kind of grooming

01:32:33.300 --> 01:32:35.113
of the skills of listening that would

01:32:35.610 --> 01:32:37.439
see them as a

01:32:38.070 --> 01:32:39.070
very

01:32:41.520 --> 01:32:43.649
important, perhaps even crucial

01:32:44.190 --> 01:32:45.300
part of

01:32:46.620 --> 01:32:49.499
education and also

01:32:50.790 --> 01:32:51.899
ethical life.

01:32:55.200 --> 01:32:56.580
That's a great answer.

01:32:56.610 --> 01:32:57.610
Thanks, Alan.

01:32:58.200 --> 01:32:59.474
And one more question from

01:33:00.750 --> 01:33:02.510
Joanne, this one is for Adam.

01:33:03.630 --> 01:33:05.369
How can you begin to judge quality of

01:33:05.370 --> 01:33:07.939
potentially scholarly annotations?

01:33:07.950 --> 01:33:10.199
Is it risky to include these in terms of your

01:33:10.200 --> 01:33:12.013
institute having to judge the quality

01:33:12.720 --> 01:33:13.720
of the scholarship?

01:33:14.920 --> 01:33:16.292
That is a good question, and

01:33:17.380 --> 01:33:18.789
it's a point at which

01:33:20.130 --> 01:33:22.449
Mindy spoke about Christopher Stewart and

01:33:22.450 --> 01:33:23.450
his, his, the

01:33:24.790 --> 01:33:26.649
importance that he placed on the truth,

01:33:27.310 --> 01:33:28.878
and some of the other folks from

01:33:29.740 --> 01:33:31.510
Galvin Library that worked on the project.

01:33:31.810 --> 01:33:33.378
Evan English is another, another

01:33:34.150 --> 01:33:36.640
former, former Galvin staffer who did a lot of

01:33:36.670 --> 01:33:38.710
a lot of work related to this and

01:33:39.760 --> 01:33:41.829
every indication that I have

01:33:42.070 --> 01:33:44.659
gotten from anyone,

01:33:44.840 --> 01:33:46.680
including Mindy is that they did this very

01:33:47.300 --> 01:33:49.489
scrupulously, there was a,

01:33:50.210 --> 01:33:51.529
there was a committee set up

01:33:52.880 --> 01:33:54.969
that was kind of the scholarly

01:33:56.340 --> 01:33:58.589
support committee for the project,

01:33:59.670 --> 01:34:01.434
and so all of, all of the commentary

01:34:02.040 --> 01:34:03.040
and all of that kind

01:34:04.590 --> 01:34:06.959
of extra, extra context

01:34:06.960 --> 01:34:09.719
that we're bringing in, we're reproducing

01:34:09.720 --> 01:34:11.339
what was on the site before.

01:34:12.660 --> 01:34:14.326
And I am aware that to some extent

01:34:15.300 --> 01:34:17.430
me saying we're taking what was there and

01:34:17.520 --> 01:34:19.199
putting it, putting it there again

01:34:19.950 --> 01:34:21.480
is a little bit of a cop out.

01:34:22.170 --> 01:34:24.326
But as as your question correctly indicates,

01:34:24.720 --> 01:34:26.879
I am certainly not in a place

01:34:27.300 --> 01:34:29.609
to feel that I can, I can judge this.

01:34:32.039 --> 01:34:33.360
And really,

01:34:35.040 --> 01:34:37.350
to some extent, we, we kind of hope

01:34:37.620 --> 01:34:40.470
that the folks who really,

01:34:40.710 --> 01:34:42.479
really know what they're talking about are

01:34:42.480 --> 01:34:44.519
going to be using the site, interacting with

01:34:44.520 --> 01:34:45.549
the site and offering

01:34:46.860 --> 01:34:48.260
suggestions and commentary.

01:34:48.990 --> 01:34:51.540
Axel Doßmann, who Alan mentioned earlier,

01:34:52.290 --> 01:34:54.450
who indeed is a wonderful

01:34:54.990 --> 01:34:57.059
resource pertaining to Boder and all of his

01:34:57.060 --> 01:34:59.050
interviews and has done some amazing work,

01:34:59.580 --> 01:35:01.259
he and I had some really interesting

01:35:01.260 --> 01:35:04.229
conversations about

01:35:04.710 --> 01:35:06.839
kind of the data we had and the site we had,

01:35:06.840 --> 01:35:09.239
and things that he was doing at his

01:35:09.240 --> 01:35:11.880
home institution and ways they were

01:35:12.390 --> 01:35:14.219
expounding upon some of what was in the

01:35:14.220 --> 01:35:15.220
interviews.

01:35:16.230 --> 01:35:17.749
And we we would love to do kind

01:35:18.720 --> 01:35:20.239
of a full bore scholarly review

01:35:21.210 --> 01:35:22.529
of all of that.

01:35:22.540 --> 01:35:24.269
We're, we're not at a place right now where we

01:35:24.270 --> 01:35:25.830
could do it, unfortunately.

01:35:25.840 --> 01:35:28.169
But, you know, in the future,

01:35:28.350 --> 01:35:29.399
perhaps we can.

01:35:29.550 --> 01:35:31.859
So really, I'm, I'm standing

01:35:31.860 --> 01:35:34.229
on the shoulders of Christopher and Evan and

01:35:34.500 --> 01:35:36.269
Mindy and Kristen and people that worked on

01:35:36.270 --> 01:35:37.446
this project previously.

01:35:37.560 --> 01:35:39.226
And I owe them a debt of gratitude

01:35:39.930 --> 01:35:41.547
as well as Stephen, who, again, I

01:35:42.330 --> 01:35:44.429
just want to point out, without his kind of

01:35:44.430 --> 01:35:46.859
enthusiasm and support, this

01:35:46.860 --> 01:35:49.560
whole version of the Voice of the Holocaust

01:35:49.860 --> 01:35:51.890
would not be, perhaps not be extant

01:35:52.230 --> 01:35:53.909
at all, or at least it wouldn't be what it

01:35:53.910 --> 01:35:56.219
was. So I just want to make

01:35:56.220 --> 01:35:57.220
sure I say that as well.

01:36:00.640 --> 01:36:01.640
Thanks, Adam.

01:36:02.590 --> 01:36:04.960
OK, we've definitely

01:36:05.110 --> 01:36:06.460
gone a little bit long.

01:36:06.610 --> 01:36:09.009
And speaking of public listening,

01:36:09.010 --> 01:36:11.590
this was an exercise in public listening.

01:36:13.060 --> 01:36:15.279
So I appreciate everyone,

01:36:16.210 --> 01:36:18.729
everyone who came and stayed with us to the,

01:36:18.760 --> 01:36:19.760
to the end.

01:36:20.260 --> 01:36:22.420
If there are any other questions you,

01:36:22.900 --> 01:36:24.801
for, for Adam or Mindy, or for Alan that you

01:36:25.480 --> 01:36:27.339
didn't feel comfortable addressing.

01:36:28.240 --> 01:36:30.219
Now you can always send them to me and I'll

01:36:30.220 --> 01:36:32.460
pass them on by email.

01:36:33.040 --> 01:36:35.469
But thank you again to Alan,

01:36:35.470 --> 01:36:37.989
and to Mindy and to Adam for all of your work.

01:36:37.990 --> 01:36:40.230
And congratulations on ten, the ten

01:36:40.450 --> 01:36:41.710
year anniversary of

01:36:43.210 --> 01:36:45.549
your book, Alan. It's still a wonderful,

01:36:46.390 --> 01:36:48.250
wonderful piece of scholarship and

01:36:48.790 --> 01:36:50.170
it continues to sort of

01:36:51.310 --> 01:36:53.920
impress our work

01:36:54.130 --> 01:36:55.780
at the Fortunoff Video Archive

01:36:56.560 --> 01:36:57.699
and impact our work.

01:36:57.700 --> 01:36:59.949
So I appreciate you being here,

01:37:00.160 --> 01:37:01.160
OK?

01:37:01.600 --> 01:37:04.239
And with that, we'll bid

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:05.320
everyone adieu.

01:37:05.740 --> 01:37:06.409
Thanks for coming.

